TP.register({id:"lp10-g8",lp:10,grade:"8",mode:"curriculum",checks:9,nextGrade:"high school",build(E){const F=E.F;const ico=(n,s=72)=>F.icon(n,s);const STORY_TITLE="Nineteen Minutes";const STORY=`<p>Toby had been told, more than once, that a pendulum clock was an unreasonable thing for a thirteen-year-old to build, and he had taken that as information rather than as advice.</p>
<p>He got it running in February. It had kept good time since March, which is to say it lost about four seconds a day, which he could live with. On the last morning of the year he carried it out to the exhibition hall on a dolly with a blanket under it, set it on the table, leveled it with two folded index cards, and started the pendulum at eight.</p>
<p>By noon it was nineteen minutes slow.</p>
<p>He checked the escapement twice. He checked the pendulum rod for a bend. He stood with both hands on the edge of the table for a while, long enough that his sister asked whether he was all right and he said yes in a voice that meant no.</p>
<p>Ms. Vance came over at half past twelve. She had run the building for twenty-two years and had watched the clock go up and come down four times since February.</p>
<p>“Floor,” she said.</p>
<p>“It is level. I checked it.”</p>
<p>“Not level. Soft.” She put one foot flat beside the table leg and pressed, and the whole table moved about a sixteenth of an inch. “Nineteen minutes is a lot of little bumps. Every person who walks past this table takes a second off you.”</p>
<p>Toby looked down the hall at the crowd, which had been building since eleven and was four deep at the far end.</p>
<p>“So I should have put it on the stage.”</p>
<p>“You could have. Then twelve people would have seen it.”</p>
<p>He did not say anything for a moment.</p>
<p>“Leave it,” she said. “Write the number on a card.”</p>
<p>So he wrote NINETEEN MINUTES SLOW BY NOON on an index card and propped it against the base, and underneath, because he could not help himself, he wrote AND I KNOW WHY.</p>
<p>People stopped. More than had stopped before. By two o'clock he had explained the floor about forty times, and by three he had stopped explaining it at all and was only asking people to stand still and watch the second hand, which they did, and which was, he decided afterwards, the one part of the whole year he would have kept exactly as it happened.</p>`;const story=()=>`<h3 style="margin:0 0 6px;color:var(--sea)">${STORY_TITLE}</h3><div class="passage sm">${STORY}</div>`;const CLOCK_TITLE="Why a Pendulum Clock Loses Time";const CLOCKTEXT=`<p>A pendulum clock does not measure time. It counts swings, and then assumes that every swing takes the same length of time. The assumption is very nearly true, which is why the design lasted three hundred years, and the places where it stops being true are the places a clock goes wrong.</p>
<p>How long one swing takes depends mostly on the length of the pendulum. A longer rod swings more slowly. A rod one meter long takes about two seconds to swing out and back. Make that rod longer by one part in a thousand and the clock loses about forty seconds a day.</p>
<p>That is why temperature matters. Steel expands as it warms, so a steel rod in a warm hall is slightly longer than the same rod in a cold one, and a clock that is right in January runs slow in June. Good clocks use a rod that barely expands, or a design in which one part lengthens downward while another part pushes the weight back up by the same amount.</p>
<p>The other way to lose time is to lose energy. A clock takes a small push from its weight or its spring on every swing, just enough to replace what friction takes away. If the case is not rigid, part of each push goes into moving the case instead of the pendulum, and the swing arrives late. A clock bolted to a masonry wall keeps better time than the same clock on a wooden shelf, and much better time than the same clock on a table that people walk past.</p>`;const clockText=()=>`<h3 style="margin:0 0 6px;color:var(--sea)">${CLOCK_TITLE}</h3><div class="passage sm">${CLOCKTEXT}</div>`;const X_SRC="Text X: Report to the Ardenvale school board from the Exhibition Committee. June 2026.";const X_TEXT=`<p>The exhibition ran from 11 a.m. to 3 p.m. with 54 tables, and it was the best attended event this school has held. Our clicker at the main door recorded 820 visitors.</p>
<p>The hall gives us 4,200 square feet, which is room for every eighth grader to have a table of their own. At noon the far end was four deep, which is what demand looks like.</p>
<p>We recommend the hall again next year, with no changes.</p>`;const Y_SRC="Text Y: Memo to the Ardenvale school board from the Facilities Office. June 2026.";const Y_TEXT=`<p>We also counted. Two staff with clickers stood at both doors for the whole four hours and logged 610 visitors. The committee's figure of 820 comes from one clicker at the main door, which cannot tell an arrival from a person stepping out and coming back in.</p>
<p>On floor area, the hall measures 4,200 square feet wall to wall, but 1,100 of that is fire lane and door clearance that cannot hold tables or people. The usable figure is 3,100.</p>
<p>We agree the far end was four deep at noon. We read that as an aisle too narrow for the number of tables, not as a measure of how many people wanted to come. The same crowd in the gymnasium, which has 6,400 usable square feet, would not have stacked up at all.</p>`;const twoTexts=()=>`<style>body:not(.sup-big) .texts article p{font-size:1.06rem;line-height:1.5}</style><div class="texts"><article><p class="src" style="margin:0 0 6px">${X_SRC}</p>${X_TEXT}</article><article><p class="src" style="margin:0 0 6px">${Y_SRC}</p>${Y_TEXT}</article></div>`;const SRC_A_CARD="Report: “Floor Vibration and Timekeeping in Free-Standing Pendulum Clocks.” By Dr. Soraya Lindqvist, horological laboratory, Deakin Institute of Metrology, 2024.";const SRC_A_TEXT="A clock placed on a suspended wooden floor with foot traffic lost a median of 21 minutes over four hours. The same clock, on the same floor, inside a rigid case bolted to a wall, lost 9 seconds. Vibration, not level, accounted for nearly all of the difference.";const SRC_B_CARD="A reply in a clock-collecting forum thread, posted by a user called tickteller. No date.";const SRC_B_TEXT="If a clock loses time it is 99% always the pendulum length, just shorten the rod a touch and youre fine. Vibration doesnt really do anything, the bob is way too heavy for footsteps to matter.";const srcCard=(tag,head,body)=>`<article style="flex:1 1 320px;border:2px solid var(--line);border-radius:16px;padding:12px 16px;background:var(--surface)"><h3 style="margin:0 0 4px;color:var(--sea)">${tag}</h3><p style="margin:0 0 8px;font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700">${head}</p><p style="margin:0;font-size:1.25rem;line-height:1.5">${body}</p></article>`;const SOURCES=()=>`<p class="big" style="font-size:1.5rem">Research question: why did the clock lose nineteen minutes in four hours?</p><div style="display:flex;gap:16px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:14px">${srcCard("Source A",SRC_A_CARD,SRC_A_TEXT)}${srcCard("Source B",SRC_B_CARD,SRC_B_TEXT)}</div>`;const checklist=items=>`<ul class="qlist" style="margin-top:14px">${items.map(x=>`<li>${x}</li>`).join("")}</ul>`;const factBox=(title,items)=>`<div style="border:3px dashed var(--line);border-radius:16px;background:var(--surface);padding:10px 20px;margin:14px 0"><p class="fig-label" style="margin:0 0 6px;text-align:left">${title}</p><ul style="margin:0;padding-left:1.2em;font-size:1.22rem;line-height:1.5">${items.map(x=>`<li>${x}</li>`).join("")}</ul></div>`;const bigTable=(head,rows,cap)=>`<div class="tool-scroll"><table class="chart" style="margin:12px auto;font-size:1.25rem">${cap?`<caption style="font-size:1.05rem;padding-bottom:6px">${cap}</caption>`:""}<tr>${head.map(h=>`<th style="text-align:center">${h}</th>`).join("")}</tr>${rows.map(r=>`<tr>${r.map((c,i)=>i?`<td style="text-align:center">${c}</td>`:`<th>${c}</th>`).join("")}</tr>`).join("")}</table></div>`;const lead=t=>`<p class="big">${t}</p>`;function plot(o){const m={l:o.yl?78:38,r:28,t:o.title?34:20,b:o.xl?56:30},sx=o.sx,sy=o.sy,W=o.x1*sx,H=o.y1*sy;const X=x=>m.l+x*sx,Y=y=>m.t+(o.y1-y)*sy,gx=o.gx||1,gy=o.gy||1,lx=o.lx||gx,ly=o.ly||gy;let s=`<svg viewBox="0 0 ${W+m.l+m.r} ${H+m.t+m.b}" width="${o.w||W+m.l+m.r}" role="img" aria-label="${o.alt}" style="max-width:100%;height:auto">`;if(o.title)s+=`<text x="${m.l+W/2}" y="21" font-size="17" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--ink)">${o.title}</text>`;s+=`<rect x="${m.l}" y="${m.t}" width="${W}" height="${H}" fill="var(--surface)"/>`;for(let x=0;x<=o.x1+1e-9;x+=gx)s+=`<line x1="${X(x)}" y1="${m.t}" x2="${X(x)}" y2="${m.t+H}" stroke="var(--line)" stroke-width="1"/>`;for(let y=0;y<=o.y1+1e-9;y+=gy)s+=`<line x1="${m.l}" y1="${Y(y)}" x2="${m.l+W}" y2="${Y(y)}" stroke="var(--line)" stroke-width="1"/>`;s+=`<line x1="${m.l}" y1="${Y(0)}" x2="${m.l+W}" y2="${Y(0)}" stroke="var(--ink)" stroke-width="2.5"/><line x1="${X(0)}" y1="${m.t}" x2="${X(0)}" y2="${m.t+H}" stroke="var(--ink)" stroke-width="2.5"/>`;for(let x=0;x<=o.x1+1e-9;x+=lx)s+=`<text x="${X(x)}" y="${Y(0)+18}" font-size="13" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--ink)">${+x.toFixed(3)}</text>`;for(let y=0;y<=o.y1+1e-9;y+=ly)s+=`<text x="${m.l-7}" y="${Y(y)}" font-size="13" text-anchor="end" dominant-baseline="central" fill="var(--ink)">${+y.toFixed(3)}</text>`;if(o.xl)s+=`<text x="${m.l+W/2}" y="${m.t+H+48}" font-size="15" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--ink)">${o.xl}</text>`;if(o.yl)s+=`<text transform="translate(20,${m.t+H/2}) rotate(-90)" font-size="15" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--ink)">${o.yl}</text>`;(o.paths||[]).forEach(p=>{s+=`<polyline points="${p.pts.map(q=>X(q[0])+","+Y(q[1])).join(" ")}" fill="none" stroke="${p.c||"var(--sea)"}" stroke-width="3.5" stroke-linejoin="round" ${p.dash?'stroke-dasharray="9 6"':""}/>`});(o.pts||[]).forEach(p=>{s+=`<circle cx="${X(p[0])}" cy="${Y(p[1])}" r="6" fill="${p[2]||"var(--coral)"}" stroke="var(--ink)" stroke-width="1.4"/>`});(o.labels||[]).forEach(l=>{s+=`<text x="${X(l[0])}" y="${Y(l[1])}" font-size="16" font-weight="700" fill="var(--ink)" text-anchor="${l[2]||"start"}" paint-order="stroke" stroke="var(--surface)" stroke-width="4">${l[3]}</text>`});return s+"</svg>"}const crewGraph=()=>plot({alt:"Graph of tables standing against hours since nine o'clock. Crew A rises in a solid straight line from 8 at zero hours to 56 at eight hours. Crew B rises in a dashed straight line from 24 at zero hours to 40 at eight hours. The two lines cross.",title:"Two crews putting up tables",x1:8,y1:60,sx:46,sy:5.4,gx:1,lx:1,gy:10,ly:10,xl:"Hours since nine o'clock",yl:"Tables standing",paths:[{pts:[[0,8],[8,56]]},{pts:[[0,24],[8,40]],c:"var(--coral)",dash:true}],labels:[[5.1,43,"start","Crew A"],[.4,28,"start","Crew B"]]});const STOPS_DATA=[[5,96],[12,90],[20,83],[28,76],[35,70],[44,62],[52,55],[60,48],[66,81],[72,37],[85,25]];const scatter=()=>plot({alt:"Scatter plot of people who stopped against distance from the main door, for eleven tables. Ten points fall steadily from upper left to lower right. One point at 66 feet sits far above the rest.",title:"Eleven tables at the exhibition",x1:90,y1:100,sx:5.2,sy:2.9,gx:10,lx:10,gy:20,ly:20,xl:"Distance from the main door, in feet",yl:"People who stopped",pts:STOPS_DATA});const T=(x,y,t,a,fs)=>`<text x="${x}" y="${y}" text-anchor="${a||"middle"}" font-size="${fs||16}" font-weight="700" fill="var(--ink)" paint-order="stroke" stroke="var(--surface)" stroke-width="3.5">${t}</text>`;const wallFig=()=>`<svg viewBox="0 0 360 230" width="360" style="max-width:100%;height:auto" role="img" aria-label="A rectangle 15 feet long and 8 feet tall with a banner drawn corner to corner across it, labeled with a question mark">
 <rect x="40" y="30" width="270" height="144" fill="var(--sea-soft)" fill-opacity=".5" stroke="var(--fig-ink)" stroke-width="3"/>
 <line x1="40" y1="174" x2="310" y2="30" stroke="var(--coral)" stroke-width="4"/>
 <path d="M52 174 v-12 h-12" fill="none" stroke="var(--fig-ink)" stroke-width="2"/>
 ${T(175,200,"15 ft")}${T(32,106,"8 ft","end")}${T(212,70,"? ft","start")}</svg>`;const rampFig=()=>`<svg viewBox="0 0 280 160" width="280" style="max-width:100%;height:auto" role="img" aria-label="A right triangle standing for a ramp, with a horizontal run of 3.6 meters, a vertical rise of 1.5 meters, and the sloping ramp surface marked with a question mark">
 <polygon points="40,120 220,120 220,45" fill="var(--gold)" fill-opacity=".4" stroke="var(--fig-ink)" stroke-width="3"/>
 <path d="M208 120 v-12 h12" fill="none" stroke="var(--fig-ink)" stroke-width="2"/>
 ${T(130,146,"3.6 m")}${T(236,86,"1.5 m","start")}${T(112,76,"? m","end")}</svg>`;const gridFig=()=>F.coordGrid({max:10,quad4:true,size:360,points:[[-4,1,"P"],[2,9,"Q"]],lines:[[[-4,1],[2,9]]]});const foldGraph=()=>plot({alt:"Graph of programs folded against minutes for Mia. A straight line rises from the origin to 75 programs at 5 minutes, with points marked at 1 minute and 15 programs and at 3 minutes and 45 programs.",title:"Mia folding programs",x1:5,y1:75,sx:60,sy:3.6,gx:1,lx:1,gy:15,ly:15,xl:"Minutes",yl:"Programs folded",paths:[{pts:[[0,0],[5,75]]}],pts:[[1,15],[3,45]]});const triGrid=()=>F.coordGrid({max:10,quad4:true,size:360,polys:[[[2,2],[6,2],[2,5]]],points:[[2,2,""],[6,2,""],[2,5,""]]});const chairTable=()=>bigTable(["Hours after 9 a.m.","0","1","2","3"],[["Chairs out","30","54","78","102"]],"The chair crew");const STOPS=[{id:1,title:"Nineteen Minutes",short:"The Clock",parts:[{std:["RL.8.1"],dur:130,dir:"Read the story to yourself. Then answer out loud. Read me the exact words that prove each answer.",desc:"citing the strongest evidence for what a story says and for inferences drawn from it",html:story,qs:["How well had the clock been keeping time before the exhibition? Read me the exact words.","Which is the strongest evidence that Toby already suspected something was wrong with the table rather than the clock? Pick A, B, or C and say why. A: “He checked the escapement twice.” B: “He stood with both hands on the edge of the table for a while.” C: “It is level. I checked it.”","The story never says Toby was upset at noon. Read me the strongest evidence that he was.","By three o'clock Toby has changed what he thinks the table is for. Read me the evidence for what he thought at noon and the evidence for what he thinks by three."],key:["It lost about four seconds a day. Reads “It had kept good time since March, which is to say it lost about four seconds a day, which he could live with.” (RL.8.1, explicit evidence)","C. “It is level. I checked it” shows he had already tested the table and had an answer ready for it, which means the table was on his list of suspects before Ms. Vance named it. A and B are about the clock and about standing still, and B in particular shows distress rather than suspicion. (RL.8.1, strongest evidence)","“his sister asked whether he was all right and he said yes in a voice that meant no.” That is stronger than the checking, because checking is what he would do either way; the voice is the detail the narrator supplies only to tell us how he felt. (RL.8.1, inference with the strongest support)","At noon he wanted the clock to be right and thought the hall was the problem: “So I should have put it on the stage.” By three the point of the table is the explaining and then the watching: “he had stopped explaining it at all and was only asking people to stand still and watch the second hand,” and he decides it is “the one part of the whole year he would have kept exactly as it happened.” (RL.8.1, two supported readings)"]}]},{id:2,title:"Roots",short:"Roots",parts:[{std:["8.EE.A.2"],dur:125,dir:"Solve on your paper and hold it up. Use the root symbol where it is asked for.",desc:"using square root and cube root symbols to solve equations, evaluating perfect squares and cubes, and knowing that the square root of 2 is irrational",html:()=>lead("The exhibition crew works in squares and cubes all day: square table tops, cube-shaped crates, and square floor tiles."),qs:["Solve x squared = 49. Give every solution.","Solve x cubed = 64. How many real solutions does it have, and why is that different from the first question?","Evaluate the square root of 121 and the cube root of 27.","A square table top has an area of 9 square feet. How long is one side? Explain why only one of the solutions to x squared = 9 is an answer here.","A cube-shaped crate has a volume of 125 cubic inches. How long is one edge?","Is the square root of 2 rational or irrational? How do you know, and between which two whole numbers does it sit?"],key:["x = 7 or x = −7. Both squares give 49. Written with the symbol, x = plus or minus the square root of 49. Partial: x = 7 only leaves the negative root out and is approaching. (8.EE.A.2)","x = 4, and that is the only real solution. Cubing keeps the sign, so a negative number cubed is negative and cannot give 64, while squaring destroys the sign, which is why x squared = 49 had two answers. Or: list the cubes to 64 and show no negative one reaches it. Partial: x = 4 with no account of the difference is approaching. (8.EE.A.2)","11 and 3, because 11 × 11 = 121 and 3 × 3 × 3 = 27. Partial: one of the two right is approaching. (8.EE.A.2, perfect squares and cubes)","3 feet. The solutions to x squared = 9 are 3 and −3, but a side length cannot be negative, so only 3 answers the question. Partial: 3 feet with no account of why −3 is rejected is approaching. (8.EE.A.2)","5 inches, because 5 × 5 × 5 = 125. Or: list the cubes to 125, or test 4 and 5. (8.EE.A.2)","Irrational. It cannot be written as a ratio of two whole numbers, and its decimal goes on for ever without ever settling into a repeating block. It sits between 1 and 2, because 1 squared is 1 and 2 squared is 4, and 2 is between them. Partial: irrational with no two whole numbers given, or the reverse, is approaching. (8.EE.A.2, knowing the square root of 2 is irrational)"]}]},{id:3,title:"Why a Clock Loses Time",short:"Pendulum",parts:[{std:["RI.8.1"],dur:125,dir:"Read the article to yourself. Then answer out loud. Read me the exact words that prove each answer.",desc:"citing the strongest evidence from an informational text for an explicit answer and for inferences",html:clockText,qs:["What does a pendulum clock actually count, and what does it assume? Read me the exact words.","Which single sentence most strongly supports the claim that a very small change in the rod can matter a great deal? Read it and say why it is stronger than the others.","Why does the article say a clock that is right in January runs slow in June? Read me the words.","A clock on a table in a busy hall loses far more time than the same clock on a shelf in a quiet room. Which sentences in the article most strongly support that, and what has to be put together to reach it?"],key:["It counts swings and assumes every swing takes the same length of time. Reads “It counts swings, and then assumes that every swing takes the same length of time.” (RI.8.1, explicit evidence)","“Make that rod longer by one part in a thousand and the clock loses about forty seconds a day.” It is the only sentence that attaches a number to both the change and the result, so the reader can see how small the cause is against how large the effect is. The sentences about longer rods swinging more slowly state the direction but not the size. (RI.8.1, strongest evidence)","Because steel expands when it warms, which makes the rod longer, and a longer rod swings more slowly. Reads “Steel expands as it warms, so a steel rod in a warm hall is slightly longer than the same rod in a cold one.” (RI.8.1)","“If the case is not rigid, part of each push goes into moving the case instead of the pendulum, and the swing arrives late,” together with “much better time than the same clock on a table that people walk past.” The reader has to join the general mechanism, energy going into the case rather than the pendulum, to the particular case of a table being knocked, which the last sentence names but does not explain. (RI.8.1, inference with the strongest support)"]}]},{id:4,title:"Across the Corner",short:"Pythagoras",parts:[{std:["8.G.B.7"],dur:140,dir:"Use the three pictures. Work on your paper and hold it up. Show the squares you added or subtracted.",desc:"applying the Pythagorean theorem in two and three dimensions and on the coordinate plane",html:()=>lead("Everything in the hall has to be measured corner to corner before it is cut, hung, or carried through a door."),fig:()=>`<div class="figure" style="flex-wrap:wrap;gap:20px;align-items:flex-start"><div style="text-align:center">${wallFig()}<p class="fig-label" style="margin:2px 0 0">Picture 1, the banner wall</p></div><div style="text-align:center">${rampFig()}<p class="fig-label" style="margin:2px 0 0">Picture 2, the ramp</p></div><div style="text-align:center">${gridFig()}<p class="fig-label" style="margin:2px 0 0">Picture 3, the floor grid</p></div></div>`,qs:["Picture 1. A banner is hung corner to corner across a wall 15 feet long and 8 feet tall. How long is the banner?","Picture 2. A ramp rises 1.5 meters over a horizontal run of 3.6 meters. How long is the sloping surface?","A rectangular table is 6 feet by 2.5 feet. How long is its diagonal?","Picture 3. Find the distance from P to Q. Use the coordinates, not a ruler.","A crate is 8 inches by 6 inches by 10 inches. What is the longest straight rod that will fit inside it, corner to corner? Give the exact answer and then a decimal.","A guy wire 25 meters long runs from the top of a 20 meter pole down to the ground. How far from the base of the pole is it anchored?"],key:["17 feet. 15 squared + 8 squared = 225 + 64 = 289, and the square root of 289 is 17. Or: recognize the 8, 15, 17 triple. Partial: 289 found with no square root taken is approaching. (8.G.B.7, finding the hypotenuse)","3.9 meters. 1.5 squared + 3.6 squared = 2.25 + 12.96 = 15.21, and the square root of 15.21 is 3.9. Or: recognize the 5, 12, 13 triple scaled by 0.3. (8.G.B.7)","6.5 feet. 6 squared + 2.5 squared = 36 + 6.25 = 42.25, and the square root of 42.25 is 6.5. Or: recognize the 5, 12, 13 triple scaled by 0.5. (8.G.B.7)","10 units. From P(−4, 1) to Q(2, 9) the horizontal leg is 2 − (−4) = 6 and the vertical leg is 9 − 1 = 8, so 36 + 64 = 100 and the distance is 10. Or: recognize the 3, 4, 5 triple scaled by 2, or use the distance formula, which is the same theorem written out. Partial: the two legs found with no distance is approaching. (8.G.B.7, distance between two points on the coordinate plane)","Exactly the square root of 200 inches, which is 10 times the square root of 2, about 14.1 inches. For a box, length squared + width squared + height squared = diagonal squared, so 64 + 36 + 100 = 200. Or: find the floor diagonal first, √100 = 10, then 10² + 10² = 200. Partial: one of the exact and the decimal answers given is approaching. (8.G.B.7, three dimensions)","15 meters. The wire is the hypotenuse, so 25 squared − 20 squared = 625 − 400 = 225, and the square root of 225 is 15. Watch for adding the squares instead of subtracting. Or: recognize the 3, 4, 5 triple scaled by 5. (8.G.B.7, finding a leg)"]}]},{id:5,title:"Leave It",short:"The Turn",parts:[{std:["RL.8.3"],dur:125,dir:"Read the story to yourself. Then answer out loud. Read me the line or the sentence you are talking about.",desc:"explaining how lines of dialogue and incidents propel the action, reveal character, and provoke a decision",html:story,qs:["Ms. Vance's first word to Toby is “Floor.” How does that one word change the scene, and what does it reveal about her?","She presses her foot beside the table leg. How does that incident move the story forward, and what does it do that her words alone could not?","“You could have. Then twelve people would have seen it.” What decision does that line provoke, and where in the story do you see the result?","Toby writes a second line on the card that nobody asked him to write. What does that reveal about him, and how does it change what the rest of the day becomes?"],key:["It cuts straight past everything he has been checking and names the cause in one word, which ends his search and starts the real scene. It reveals a person who has watched this building for twenty-two years and does not spend words: she has already diagnosed it from across the hall, and she expects to be argued with, which she is. (RL.8.3, dialogue propels the action and reveals character)","It settles the argument by demonstration rather than assertion. He has just said “It is level. I checked it,” so a claim would have met another claim. The sixteenth of an inch is something he can see, and it converts a disagreement into an explanation he can then pass on to other people all afternoon. It also reframes the crowd from an audience into a cause. (RL.8.3, an incident propels the action)","It provokes him to stop wanting the clock to have been somewhere else. She trades his imagined fix against what it would have cost, twelve viewers instead of a hall, and he goes quiet: “He did not say anything for a moment.” The result is that he accepts “Leave it,” writes the card, and spends the afternoon with the clock exactly where it is. (RL.8.3, dialogue provokes a decision)","“AND I KNOW WHY” is pride, and the narrator says he could not help himself, so it is also honesty about his own pride. It turns a confession of failure into a claim of understanding, which is why people stop: the card is now an invitation. That is what makes the last paragraph possible, where explaining gives way to people standing and watching with him. (RL.8.3, an incident reveals character and shapes what follows)"]}]},{id:6,title:"Does It Repeat",short:"Rationals",parts:[{std:["8.NS.A.1"],dur:110,dir:"Work on your paper and hold it up. Explain each decision, do not just sort.",desc:"telling rational numbers from irrational ones, and converting a repeating decimal into a fraction",html:()=>lead("The crew measures everything twice, once with a tape and once with a calculator, and the calculator keeps producing decimals that do not stop."),qs:["Sort these as rational or irrational, and say why for each one: 0.75, the square root of 9, the square root of 10, pi, 5/8, 0.3333 repeating.","Write 7/11 as a decimal. What do you notice?","Convert 0.4444 repeating into a fraction.","Convert 0.272727 repeating into a fraction.","Why must every rational number have a decimal that either ends or repeats? Explain using long division.","Name a number between 3 and 4 that is irrational, and say how you know it is."],key:["Rational: 0.75, because it is 3/4; the square root of 9, because it is 3; 5/8, because it is already a ratio of whole numbers; and 0.3333 repeating, because it is 1/3. Irrational: the square root of 10, because 10 is not a perfect square and its root cannot be written as a ratio of whole numbers; and pi, whose decimal never ends and never repeats. Partial: the six sorted right with the reasons thin is approaching, and so is one misplaced number with the reasoning sound. (8.NS.A.1)","0.636363 repeating, which is 0.63 with the 63 repeating for ever. It repeats rather than ends, and the repeating block has two digits. Or: 7/11 as 7 × 1/11, and 1/11 is 0.0909... (8.NS.A.1, the decimal expansion of a rational number)","4/9. Let x = 0.4444 repeating. Then 10x = 4.4444 repeating, so 10x − x = 4, which gives 9x = 4 and x = 4/9. Or: name it from the pattern, since 0.1111 repeating is 1/9. Partial: 4/9 with no steps shown is approaching. (8.NS.A.1, converting a repeating decimal to a fraction)","3/11. Let x = 0.272727 repeating. The block is two digits, so multiply by 100: 100x = 27.272727 repeating. Then 100x − x = 27, so 99x = 27 and x = 27/99 = 3/11. Or: name it from the pattern, since a two-digit repeat sits over 99. Partial: 27/99 not reduced still counts; the method is what is rated. (8.NS.A.1)","Dividing a by b by long division, the only possible remainders are 0, 1, 2, and so on up to b − 1, which is a limited list. If a remainder of 0 turns up the decimal ends. Otherwise, within at most b steps a remainder has to repeat, and once a remainder repeats the digits that follow it repeat too, for ever. Partial: naming the limited list of remainders with no account of what happens when one repeats is approaching. (8.NS.A.1, why every rational number repeats or terminates)","Any correct one with a reason, such as pi, which is about 3.14159 and never repeats, or the square root of 10, which is about 3.162 and is irrational because 10 lies between the perfect squares 9 and 16 and is not itself a perfect square. The square root of 11 or of 12 works too. Partial: a correct number with no reason is approaching. (8.NS.A.1)"]}]},{id:7,title:"Word by Word",short:"Word Choice",parts:[{std:["RL.8.4"],dur:120,dir:"Read the story to yourself. Then answer out loud. Read me the words you are talking about.",desc:"the meaning of words and phrases, including connotation, figurative language, and the effect of specific word choices",html:story,qs:["Toby had been told a pendulum clock was “an unreasonable thing for a thirteen-year-old to build,” and he “had taken that as information rather than as advice.” What is the difference between those two words here, and what does the choice tell you about him?","Ms. Vance says, “Not level. Soft.” Compare what soft suggests in this sentence with what a word such as flexible or springy would suggest. Why does soft fit her?","“he said yes in a voice that meant no.” Why does the author write it that way instead of saying he was upset?","The last sentence calls the afternoon “the one part of the whole year he would have kept exactly as it happened.” What is the effect of the words exactly as it happened, given everything that went wrong?"],key:["Information is a fact to be used; advice is a recommendation to be followed. Taking it as information means he heard the difficulty and treated it as a specification rather than as a reason to stop. The choice tells you he is stubborn in a particular, practical way, and the dry phrasing tells you the older Toby finds that funny about himself. (RL.8.4, connotative meaning)","Flexible or springy would be neutral descriptions of a material. Soft carries weakness and unreliability, and it is also what you would say about ground rather than a floor, which is exactly the point: the floor is behaving like earth under a foot. It fits her because she speaks in single words that do a job, and soft diagnoses and judges the floor at the same time. (RL.8.4, connotation and word choice)","Saying he was upset would tell the reader a conclusion. “A voice that meant no” gives the evidence instead, and it puts the reader in the sister's position, hearing the gap between the word and the voice. It is also in keeping with a story about an instrument that reports faithfully: the voice reports accurately even though the word does not. (RL.8.4, the effect of a specific word choice)","They refuse to tidy the day up. The clock failed, the floor was wrong, and the sentence does not say he would have kept the explaining or the result but the thing exactly as it happened, failure included. Exactly is the kind of word a person who builds clocks would reach for, and using it about a day that went wrong is the story's final joke and its point at once. (RL.8.4, analyzing word choice)"]}]},{id:8,title:"Two Crews",short:"Systems",parts:[{std:["8.EE.C.8"],dur:150,dir:"Use the graph for number 1. Solve the rest on your paper and hold it up. Check every answer in both equations.",desc:"solving pairs of simultaneous linear equations by graph and by algebra, including word problems and the number of solutions",html:()=>lead("Two crews set up tables all morning. Crew A starts at nine o'clock with 8 already standing. Crew B starts at the same time with 24 already standing."),fig:()=>`<div class="figure">${crewGraph()}</div>`,qs:["At what point do the two lines cross? What does that point mean for the two crews?","Solve this pair with algebra: y = 4x − 5 and y = −3x + 16.","Solve this pair with algebra: x + y = 54 and x − y = 12.","Without solving all the way, say how many solutions each pair has and how you know. (a) y = 3x − 2 and y = 3x + 5. (b) 4x − 6y = 12 and 2x − 3y = 6.","The hall holds 54 tables, some 6 feet long and some 8 feet long, and they come to 388 feet of table in all. Write a pair of equations and find how many of each.","Crew A starts at nine o'clock and puts up 6 tables an hour. Crew C starts at ten o'clock and puts up 10 an hour. Write a pair of equations and find when Crew C has put up as many as Crew A, and how many that is."],key:["(4, 32). Four hours after nine o'clock, which is one o'clock, both crews have 32 tables standing. Partial: the crossing point read right with no account of what it means is approaching. (8.EE.C.8a)","x = 3 and y = 7, or (3, 7). Steps: 4x − 5 = −3x + 16, so 7x = 21 and x = 3, then y = 4(3) − 5 = 7. Check in the other equation: −3(3) + 16 = 7. Or: substitution, elimination, or graphing both lines all count. Partial: x = 3 with no y is approaching. (8.EE.C.8b)","x = 33 and y = 21, or (33, 21). Steps: add the two equations to get 2x = 66, so x = 33, then 33 + y = 54 gives y = 21. Check: 33 − 21 = 12. Or: substitution, y = 54 − x into x − y = 12, reaches the same pair. Partial: x = 33 with no y is approaching. (8.EE.C.8b)","(a) No solution. Both slopes are 3 and the y-intercepts differ, so the lines are parallel and never meet. Substituting gives −2 = 5, which is never true. (b) Infinitely many solutions. Multiplying the second equation by 2 gives 4x − 6y = 12, which is the first equation, so they are the same line. For example (3, 0) satisfies both. Partial: one of the two pairs answered is approaching. (8.EE.C.8b)","22 six-foot tables and 32 eight-foot tables. Equations: s + e = 54 and 6s + 8e = 388. Steps: s = 54 − e, so 6(54 − e) + 8e = 388, which gives 324 + 2e = 388, so 2e = 64 and e = 32, then s = 22. Check: 22 + 32 = 54 and 6(22) + 8(32) = 132 + 256 = 388. Or: elimination, multiplying the first equation by 6 and subtracting, or guess and check against both conditions. Partial: a correct pair of equations with the solving unfinished is approaching. (8.EE.C.8c)","At half past eleven, with 15 tables each. With t as hours after nine o'clock: n = 6t and n = 10(t − 1). Steps: 6t = 10t − 10, so 4t = 10 and t = 2.5, which is 11:30, and n = 6(2.5) = 15. Check: 10(2.5 − 1) = 15. Or: build a table of the two counts half hour by half hour and look for the row where they match. Partial: the equations right with the time or the count missing is approaching. (8.EE.C.8c)"]}]},{id:9,title:"Where They Disagree",short:"Conflict",parts:[{std:["RI.8.9"],dur:135,dir:"Read both texts to yourself. They are about the same exhibition. Then answer out loud.",desc:"analyzing two texts that conflict and identifying where they disagree on fact and where on interpretation",html:twoTexts,qs:["Name one point where the two texts disagree about a matter of fact. What exactly does each one say, and what is the reason for the gap?","Name a second factual disagreement, and say whether the two numbers actually contradict each other.","Name a point where they disagree about interpretation rather than fact. Explain the disagreement.","What evidence would settle one of the factual disagreements? Why could no evidence settle the interpretive one?"],key:["The visitor count. The committee's clicker at the main door recorded 820. Facilities put two staff with clickers at both doors for all four hours and logged 610. Facilities names the reason for the gap: one clicker at one door “cannot tell an arrival from a person stepping out and coming back in,” so the higher number would double-count re-entries. (RI.8.9, disagreement on a matter of fact)","Floor area. The committee says the hall gives 4,200 square feet. Facilities says it measures 4,200 wall to wall but that 1,100 of that is fire lane and door clearance, leaving 3,100 usable. These two do not strictly contradict: both say 4,200 for the room. They disagree about which figure answers the question, which makes it a dispute about what counts as floor the exhibition has. (RI.8.9, a second factual disagreement)","Both agree the far end was four deep at noon. The committee reads that as demand: “which is what demand looks like.” Facilities reads the same fact as a symptom of an aisle too narrow for 54 tables, and points out that the same crowd in a 6,400 square foot gymnasium “would not have stacked up at all.” The fact is shared; what it is evidence of is not. (RI.8.9, disagreement on interpretation)","The count could be settled by running both methods at once, two clickers at both doors plus the single main-door clicker, on the same day, and comparing, or by clicking exits as well as entries so re-entries can be subtracted. The floor area could be settled by measuring the fire lane and the door clearance together and agreeing in advance whether usable area or wall-to-wall area is the figure in question. No evidence settles the interpretive disagreement, because whether a four-deep crowd is a success or a bottleneck depends on what the exhibition is for, and no measurement decides that. (RI.8.9, what each disagreement rests on)"]}]},{id:10,title:"Far From the Door",short:"Scatter",parts:[{std:["8.SP.A.1"],dur:130,dir:"Use the scatter plot. Answer out loud, and name what you looked at for each answer.",desc:"constructing and interpreting a scatter plot, and describing clustering, outliers, association, and linear association",html:()=>lead("Eleven tables were tracked all afternoon. For each one the crew recorded how far it stood from the main door and how many people stopped at it."),fig:()=>`<div class="figure">${scatter()}</div>`,qs:["What does one point on this plot stand for? Name what each axis measures.","Describe the association between the two quantities. Is it positive or negative, and is it linear or not?","Is there an outlier? Give its coordinates and say how far it sits from where the pattern would put it.","The table at 66 feet is the one with the pendulum clock on it. Does that explain the outlier? Does explaining it mean you should remove it from the plot?","Ignoring the outlier, estimate how many people would have stopped at a table 40 feet from the door, and say how you got your estimate.","Does this plot show that standing near the door causes more people to stop? Explain."],key:["One point is one table, placed at that table's distance from the main door and that table's number of people who stopped. The horizontal axis is distance from the main door in feet, and the vertical axis is the number of people who stopped. Partial: what a point stands for with only one axis named is approaching. (8.SP.A.1, constructing and interpreting a scatter plot)","Negative and strongly linear. As distance goes up the number who stopped goes steadily down, and ten of the eleven points sit very close to a single straight line falling from about 96 at 5 feet to about 25 at 85 feet. Partial: negative named with nothing said about linear, or the reverse, is approaching. (8.SP.A.1, positive or negative association, linear association)","Yes, the point at (66, 81). The line through the other ten points is at roughly 42 people at 66 feet, so this table drew about 39 more people than its distance would predict. It is the only point far off the line. Partial: the point named with no account of how far off the pattern it sits is approaching. (8.SP.A.1, outliers)","It explains it: the clock table had a reason for people to stop that had nothing to do with where it stood, so it is not following the same rule as the rest. Explaining it does not mean removing it. An outlier with a known cause is evidence, not an error, and dropping it would hide the one finding on the plot that a school could act on. The honest treatment is to keep it, label it, and leave it out only of a line fitted to the other ten. Partial: explaining the outlier and then saying to remove it is approaching, not proficient. (8.SP.A.1, outliers interpreted in context)","About 65 people. The ten points fall by roughly 71 people over 80 feet, which is about 0.9 of a person per foot, so from 96 at 5 feet the estimate at 40 feet is about 96 − 0.9 × 35, which is about 65, and reading the line off the plot gives about the same. Any estimate between about 60 and 70 with the reasoning shown is correct. Or: draw a line through the ten points and read it at 40 feet. Partial: a number in range with no reasoning shown is approaching. (8.SP.A.1, using the pattern to estimate)","No. The plot shows a strong association, not a cause. Distance from the door may be the reason, but the crew may also have put the tables they expected to be popular nearest the door, or people may simply run out of time and attention as they go. The clock table is the evidence against a simple cause: at 66 feet it drew 81 people because of what was on it. Partial: no with no alternative explanation offered is approaching. (8.SP.A.1, association is not causation)"]}]},{id:11,title:"Verbals and Voice",short:"Grammar",parts:[{std:["L.8.1"],dur:120,dir:"A verbal is a verb form doing another job. Answer out loud. When you fix a sentence, say the whole sentence the correct way.",desc:"verbals, active and passive voice, mood, and fixing a shift in voice or mood",qs:["“Leveling the clock took two folded index cards.” Which word is the verbal, what kind is it, and what job does it do in the sentence?","“Ms. Vance, pressing her foot beside the table leg, said nothing.” Find the verbal and name its kind. Who or what does it describe?","“His plan was to start the pendulum at eight.” Find the verbal and name its kind.","Say this sentence in the active voice: “The card was propped against the base by Toby.”","Fix the verb: “If the floor was solid, the clock would have kept time.”","Fix the shift in voice: “Toby wrote the card, and then the clock was watched by forty people.”","Name the mood of this sentence and say what tells you: “Write the number on a card.”"],key:["Leveling. Gerund. With its phrase, “Leveling the clock,” it is the subject of the sentence, so it acts as a noun. (L.8.1a, verbals)","pressing. Participle. It describes Ms. Vance, so it acts as an adjective. (L.8.1a, verbals)","to start. Infinitive. (L.8.1a, verbals)","Toby propped the card against the base. (L.8.1b, active and passive voice)","If the floor were solid, the clock would have kept time. A condition contrary to fact takes were. (L.8.1c, subjunctive mood)","Toby wrote the card, and then forty people watched the clock. Both verbs are now active. (L.8.1d, shift in voice)","Imperative. It gives a command, and its subject, you, is understood rather than stated. (L.8.1c, mood)"]}]},{id:12,title:"Source Desk",short:"Sources",parts:[{std:["W.8.8"],n:4,label:"checks met",dur:125,dir:"Read both sources. Answer numbers 1 and 2 out loud. Write numbers 3 and 4 on your paper and hold it up.",desc:"judging sources, choosing search terms, fixing plagiarism, and citing",html:()=>SOURCES()+`<ol class="qlist"><li>The two sources disagree about what makes a clock lose time. Which source would you trust? Give two reasons from the source information, and name one thing Source A does not tell you.</li><li>You want one more source. Which search terms are best, and why? A: clocks. B: pendulum clock timekeeping floor vibration foot traffic study. C: how to fix a slow clock.</li><li>Spot the plagiarism. A student report says, with no quotation marks and no source named: <em>Vibration, not level, accounted for nearly all of the difference.</em> Fix it on your paper. Quote it correctly, or paraphrase it and name the author.</li><li>Write a citation for Source A with the author, title, publisher and year.</li></ol>`,key:["Source A. Two reasons, such as: it names an author with a relevant specialism, it comes from a named institute, it carries a date, and it reports a measured comparison in which only the mounting changed. Source B is an undated forum reply by an unknown user who writes “99% always” with no evidence. For the teacher: Source B is also wrong on the mechanism, since shortening the rod would make a vibrating clock run fast on quiet days without touching the real cause. One thing Source A does not tell you: how heavy the clock was, how many people walked past per hour, or anything about this particular hall. (W.8.8, credibility and accuracy)","B. It names the two things the question actually joins, a pendulum clock's timekeeping and vibration from foot traffic, so the results will be about that rather than about clocks in general. A is far too broad and C assumes the cause the question is trying to find. (W.8.8, search terms)","Either fix earns the check. Quote: Dr. Soraya Lindqvist reports that “vibration, not level, accounted for nearly all of the difference.” Paraphrase: according to Dr. Soraya Lindqvist, almost all of the gap between the two placements came from vibration rather than from how level the clock was. The student must name the source either way, and a quotation must carry quotation marks. (W.8.8, quoting or paraphrasing without plagiarism)","All four parts present. Example: Lindqvist, Soraya. “Floor Vibration and Timekeeping in Free-Standing Pendulum Clocks.” Deakin Institute of Metrology, 2024. Any clear, consistent order is fine. (W.8.8, citation)"]}]},{id:13,title:"Brief the Board",short:"Brief",parts:[{std:["SL.8.4"],n:5,label:"checks met",dur:120,dir:"Plan for 1 minute. Then talk for 1 to 2 minutes. Look at the camera and speak clearly.",desc:"a 1 to 2 minute spoken briefing that presents a claim and findings with evidence from a data table",html:()=>lead("The board has to decide where next year's exhibition goes. Your survey produced the table below. Two of the figures are disputed, and the table says so.")+bigTable(["","The hall","The gymnasium"],[["Floor area, wall to wall","4,200 sq ft","7,900 sq ft"],["Usable floor area","3,100 sq ft, per Facilities. The Exhibition Committee uses 4,200.","6,400 sq ft"],["Tables it held this year","54","not used this year"],["Visitors","Disputed: 820 by the committee's single door clicker, 610 by the Facilities count at both doors","not used this year"],["Crowding at noon","Four deep at the far end. Both reports agree on this.","not used this year"],["Floor","Suspended wood. Measured to move about a sixteenth of an inch underfoot.","Sprung wood over concrete. No measurement taken."],["Also worth knowing","Opens straight off the main corridor","Has to be unlocked and staffed, and is booked for exams until 4 June"]],"Two rooms the exhibition could use")+lead("Brief the board. Say which room to use, give your strongest evidence from the table, name the drawback of your own choice, and end with what you need the board to do.")+`<div class="row center" style="margin-top:16px;gap:14px;flex-wrap:wrap">${["My recommendation","The evidence","The drawback","What I need from the board"].map(x=>`<span class="pill" style="font-size:1.1rem">${x}</span>`).join("")}</div>`,key:["States a clear recommendation up front and stays focused on it. Either room can be recommended. The gymnasium case rests on 6,400 usable square feet against 3,100, and on the crowding at noon. The hall case rests on opening straight off the main corridor, on 54 tables having fitted this year, and on the gym being booked until 4 June. (SL.8.4)","Emphasizes the most important points in a focused, coherent order rather than reading the whole table out (SL.8.4)","Backs the recommendation with relevant evidence and sound reasoning, with the numbers used accurately and the disputed ones treated as disputed rather than picked (SL.8.4)","Uses well-chosen details, including the drawback of the student's own choice. For the gym: the booking until 4 June, the staffing, and the fact that no floor measurement was taken there. For the hall: 3,100 usable square feet and a floor that moves a sixteenth of an inch (SL.8.4)","Appropriate eye contact with the camera, adequate volume, and clear pronunciation for 1 to 2 minutes (SL.8.4)"]}]},{id:14,title:"Field Desk",short:"Write",parts:[{alt:{group:"write",key:"argument",label:"Argue",icon:"⚖️",hint:"The hall or the gymnasium?"},std:["W.8.1"],n:7,label:"checks met",dur:210,dir:"Write on your paper for about 5 minutes. Then hold it up or read it to your teacher.",desc:"an argument writing piece built from a fact box",html:()=>lead("The board can put next year's exhibition in one of two rooms. Write an argument for the one it should choose. Use the fact box. Write 8 to 10 sentences.")+factBox("Fact box: two rooms",["<strong>The hall.</strong> 4,200 square feet wall to wall. Facilities puts the usable figure at 3,100 after fire lane and door clearance; the Exhibition Committee uses 4,200. It held 54 tables this year. It opens straight off the main corridor.","<strong>The gymnasium.</strong> 7,900 square feet wall to wall, 6,400 usable. It has to be unlocked and staffed, and it is booked for exams until 4 June.","<strong>Visitors are disputed.</strong> The committee's single clicker at the main door recorded 820. Facilities, with two staff at both doors for four hours, logged 610. Facilities says one door clicker cannot separate an arrival from a re-entry.","Both reports agree the far end of the hall was four deep at noon. The committee reads that as demand. Facilities reads it as an aisle too narrow for 54 tables.","The hall floor is suspended wood and was measured moving about a sixteenth of an inch underfoot. No floor measurement was taken in the gymnasium.","The room cannot be split. One room has to hold the whole exhibition."])+checklist(["Introduce your claim and acknowledge the opposing claim, showing how your claim is different","Back your claim with logical reasoning and relevant evidence from the fact box","Treat the disputed figures as disputed rather than choosing the one that suits you","Use words, phrases and clauses that show how your reasons connect: because, however, as a result","Keep a formal style with no slang","End with a conclusion that follows from and supports your argument"]),key:["Introduces the claim clearly and acknowledges the opposing claim, showing how the two differ (W.8.1a)","Organizes the reasons and evidence in an order that makes sense (W.8.1a)","Supports the claim with logical reasoning and relevant evidence from the fact box, such as 6,400 usable square feet against 3,100, or the exam booking until 4 June (W.8.1b)","Uses the fact box accurately and names the disputed visitor figures as disputed rather than quoting one as settled (W.8.1b)","Uses words, phrases and clauses to connect the claim, the reasons, the evidence and the opposing claim (W.8.1c)","Establishes and maintains a formal style and an objective tone, with no slang (W.8.1d)","Ends with a concluding statement that follows from and supports the argument (W.8.1e)"]},{alt:{group:"write",key:"explain",label:"Explain",icon:"📘",hint:"Why the clock lost nineteen minutes"},std:["W.8.2"],n:7,label:"checks met",dur:210,dir:"Write on your paper for about 5 minutes. Then hold it up or read it to your teacher.",desc:"an informative writing piece built from an article and a source",html:()=>lead("Write to explain to next year's eighth graders why a pendulum clock on an exhibition table loses time, and what they could do about it. Use the article. Write 10 to 12 sentences.")+checklist(["Introduce the topic clearly and preview what is coming","Organize the causes into clear categories so one leads to the next","Develop each one with facts, definitions and concrete details from the article","Use transitions that name the relationship between ideas","Use precise language and define every technical term you use","Keep a formal style","End with a conclusion that follows from and supports the explanation"])+`<details class="look-back" open><summary>Look back at the article</summary>${clockText()}</details>`,key:["Introduces the topic clearly, previews what follows, and organizes the causes into categories (W.8.2a)","Develops the topic with relevant facts, definitions, concrete details and examples from the article (W.8.2b)","Distinguishes the two families of cause, a change in the rod's length and a loss of energy to a non-rigid mounting, rather than running them together (W.8.2b)","Uses appropriate transitions to create cohesion and clarify the relationships among ideas (W.8.2c)","Uses precise language and domain-specific vocabulary, such as pendulum, rigid, friction, and explains each term used (W.8.2d)","Establishes and maintains a formal style (W.8.2e)","Provides a concluding statement that follows from and supports the information presented (W.8.2f)"]}]},{id:15,title:"Powers and Rates",short:"Powers",parts:[{title:"Integer Exponents",std:["8.EE.A.1"],n:3,dur:45,dir:"Work on your paper. Say each answer out loud.",desc:"applying the properties of integer exponents to write equivalent expressions",html:()=>`<ol class="qlist" style="font-size:1.4rem"><li>Write 3<sup>2</sup> &times; 3<sup>&minus;5</sup> as a single power, then as a fraction.</li><li>Simplify 2<sup>4</sup> &divide; 2<sup>7</sup>.</li><li>What is 5<sup>0</sup>, and why?</li></ol>`,key:["3^-3, which is 1/3^3 = 1/27. Adding the exponents: 2 + (-5) = -3. Or: 9 ÷ 243 = 1/27 worked out as numbers.","2^-3, which is 1/8. Subtracting the exponents: 4 - 7 = -3. Or: 16 ÷ 128 = 1/8 worked out as numbers.","1. Any non-zero number to the power 0 is 1, because 5^n divided by 5^n is both 5^0 and 1. Or: step down the powers, 125, 25, 5, each step dividing by 5, so after 5 comes 1."]},{title:"Two Folders",std:["8.EE.B.5"],n:3,dur:55,dir:"Look at the graph. Answer out loud.",desc:"interpreting the unit rate as the slope of a proportional graph and comparing two relationships shown in different ways",html:()=>lead("Mia's folding is on the graph. Noor folds programs at the rate p = 12t, where t is minutes and p is programs."),fig:()=>F.row(foldGraph()),qs:["What is Mia's unit rate, and where do you see it on the graph?","Who folds faster, and how do you know, when one is a graph and the other is an equation?","Why does Mia's line pass through the origin?"],key:["Say: What is Mia's unit rate, and where do you see it on the graph? (15 programs a minute. It is the slope: the line rises 15 for every 1 across, and the point (1, 15) is the unit rate itself.) Or: read the rise over the run between any two points on the line. Partial: 15 with no place on the graph pointed to is approaching.","Say: Who folds faster, and how do you know, when one is a graph and the other is an equation? (Mia. Her slope is 15 programs a minute and Noor's equation gives 12 programs a minute, so the comparison is between the two rates, not between a picture and a formula.) Or: graph Noor's equation on the same axes and compare the steepness. Partial: the right person with no two rates compared is approaching.","Say: Why does Mia's line pass through the origin? (Because at zero minutes zero programs are folded. A proportional relationship has no starting amount, so its graph goes through (0, 0).)"]}]},{id:16,title:"Is It a Function",short:"Functions",parts:[{std:["8.F.A.1","8.F.A.3"],n:3,dur:60,dir:"Look at the table. Answer out loud.",desc:"deciding whether a rule is a function, writing it as y = mx + b, and naming a function that is not linear",html:()=>lead("The chair crew starts at nine and keeps putting chairs out.")+chairTable(),qs:["Does this table define the number of chairs as a function of the hours? Why? And would it still be a function if one hour in the table had two different chair counts next to it?","Write an equation for the chairs, say what each number means, and say whether it is linear.","Give an example of a function that is not linear, and say how you know."],key:["Say: Does this table define the number of chairs as a function of the hours? Why? And would it still be a function if one hour in the table had two different chair counts next to it? (Yes, because each input hour has exactly one output. No to the second: a function has to assign exactly one output to each input, so two outputs for one hour breaks it. 8.F.A.1). Partial: one of the two questions answered is approaching.","Say: Write an equation for the chairs, say what each number means, and say whether it is linear. (c = 24h + 30. The 24 is the rate of change, 24 chairs an hour, which is the slope. The 30 is the number already out at h = 0, which is the vertical intercept. It is linear because the chairs go up by the same 24 every hour, so the graph is a straight line. 8.F.A.3). Partial: the equation right with one of the two numbers unexplained, or with no answer about linearity, is approaching.","Say: Give an example of a function that is not linear, and say how you know. (Any correct one. The area of a square table top as a function of its side, A = s^2, gives (1, 1), (2, 4) and (3, 9), which are not on one straight line, because the jumps get bigger instead of staying the same. 8.F.A.3). Partial: a correct example with no reason is approaching."]}]},{id:17,title:"Move the Triangle",short:"Transform",parts:[{std:["8.G.A.3"],n:4,dur:75,dir:"Look at the grid. Work on your paper and say the three new points each time.",desc:"describing the effect of a translation, a reflection, a rotation and a dilation on a figure using coordinates",html:()=>lead("Triangle T is the floor plan of one display stand. Its vertices are (2, 2), (6, 2) and (2, 5)."),fig:()=>F.row(triGrid()),qs:["Translate T three units left and four units down. Give the three new vertices.","Go back to the original T. Reflect it across the x-axis. Give the three new vertices.","Go back to the original T again. Rotate it 90 degrees counterclockwise about the origin. Give the rule for what happens to a point, and give the three new vertices.","Go back to the original T once more. Dilate it from the origin by a scale factor of 2. Give the vertices, and say what happens to the side lengths and to the angles."],key:["Say: Translate T three units left and four units down. Give the three new vertices. ((-1, -2), (3, -2) and (-1, 1). A translation subtracts 3 from every x and 4 from every y, and the size and shape do not change.) Or: count the squares on the grid instead of using the rule. Partial: two of the three vertices right is approaching.","Say: Go back to the original T. Reflect it across the x-axis. Give the three new vertices. ((2, -2), (6, -2) and (2, -5). A reflection across the x-axis changes the sign of every y and leaves every x alone.) Partial: the three vertices right with nothing said about the coordinates is approaching.","Say: Go back to the original T again. Rotate it 90 degrees counterclockwise about the origin. Give the rule for what happens to a point, and give the three new vertices. (The rule is (x, y) goes to (-y, x). (2, 2) goes to (-2, 2), (6, 2) goes to (-2, 6) and (2, 5) goes to (-5, 2). Check one: (6, 2) has y = 2, so the image starts with -2, and x = 6, so it ends with 6. A rotation keeps every side length and every angle, so T is congruent to its image.) Or: trace T and turn the paper a quarter turn to the left, or call it a 270 degree clockwise turn. Partial: the rule right with a vertex wrong, or three right vertices with no rule, is approaching.","Say: Go back to the original T once more. Dilate it from the origin by a scale factor of 2. Give the vertices, and say what happens to the side lengths and to the angles. ((4, 4), (12, 4) and (4, 10). Every coordinate doubles, so every side length doubles, and the angles do not change at all. That is what makes a dilation different from the other three.) Partial: the vertices and the side lengths right with nothing said about the angles is approaching."]}]},{id:18,title:"Weigh the Argument",short:"Evaluate",parts:[{std:["RI.8.8"],n:3,dur:60,dir:"Use the two sources and Text X. Answer out loud.",desc:"delineating and evaluating an argument, judging the evidence, and spotting evidence that is beside the point",html:()=>SOURCES()+`<div class="texts"><article><p class="src" style="margin:0 0 6px">${X_SRC}</p>${X_TEXT}</article></div>`,qs:["Delineate Source B's argument. What is the claim, and what reasons are offered for it?","Is that reasoning sound, given Source A? Which reason does Source A contradict directly?","Text X argues for using the hall again with no changes. Name one thing it offers that does not bear on whether the room is suitable, and say why."],key:["Say: Delineate Source B's argument. What is the claim, and what reasons are offered for it? (Claim: a clock that loses time is almost always wrong in its pendulum length, so shorten the rod. Reasons: vibration does not really do anything, and the bob is too heavy for footsteps to matter. No evidence is offered for either reason.)","Say: Is that reasoning sound, given Source A? Which reason does Source A contradict directly? (Not sound. Source A put the same clock on the same floor two ways: with foot traffic it lost a median of 21 minutes in four hours, and in a rigid case bolted to a wall it lost 9 seconds, with vibration accounting for nearly all the difference. That is a measurement against Source B's 'vibration doesnt really do anything', and it comes from a named laboratory rather than an unnamed forum post.)","Say: Text X argues for using the hall again with no changes. Name one thing it offers that does not bear on whether the room is suitable, and say why. (Any of these, with a reason: 'the best attended event this school has held' is about the event, not about the room; the 820 figure rests on one clicker at one door and the Facilities memo disputes it, so it cannot be quoted as settled; and reading 'four deep at the far end' as demand treats a crowding measurement as an attendance measurement, which is the very point the two reports disagree about. Do not let the student settle the visitor count or the usable floor area: both are disputed and this question is about relevance, not about which number is right.)"]}]},{id:19,title:"The Scene at the Table",short:"Narrative",parts:[{std:["W.8.3"],n:5,label:"checks met",dur:75,dir:"Write on your paper. Then hold it up or read it to your teacher.",desc:"a short narrative with a context, a sequence, dialogue or description, and a reflective ending",html:()=>lead("Write a short scene in which somebody at an exhibition finds out that something they built is not working the way they expected, and decides what to do about it. It can be real or invented. Write 5 or 6 sentences.")+checklist(["Set up a context and a point of view, and introduce whoever is in it","Put the events in an order that unfolds naturally","Use dialogue or description to develop the moment","Use transitions that show time moving","End by reflecting on what happened, not just stopping"]),key:["Engages the reader by establishing a context and a point of view and introducing a narrator or characters (W.8.3a)","Organizes an event sequence that unfolds naturally and logically (W.8.3a)","Uses narrative techniques such as dialogue, pacing or description to develop the events and the characters (W.8.3b)","Uses transition words, phrases or clauses to convey sequence and signal shifts in time or setting (W.8.3c)","Uses precise words and sensory detail, and ends with a conclusion that reflects on the narrated events (W.8.3d, W.8.3e)","Five or six sentences is enough. This is a short check of narrative craft, not a full draft, so rate the moves the student makes rather than the length."]}]},{id:20,title:"Argued in Print",short:"In Print",parts:[{std:["HSS-8.1"],n:3,dur:95,dir:"Look at the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"analyzing how printed argument moved the colonies toward independence",html:()=>`<p class="big">In January 1776 a recently arrived Englishman named Thomas Paine published a pamphlet called Common Sense.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th></th><th></th></tr>
     <tr><td>Price</td><td>two shillings, cheap enough for a tradesman</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Copies sold</td><td>perhaps 150,000 in the first year, in a population of about 2.5 million</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Language</td><td>plain, with no Latin and no legal citations</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Argument</td><td>not that the king had governed badly, but that hereditary monarchy itself was absurd, and that an island could not permanently rule a continent</td></tr></table>
     <p class="big">There were about 40 newspapers in the colonies, and taverns and coffeehouses read them aloud to people who could not read.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("books",74)}${ico("pencil",74)}${ico("star",74)}</div>`,qs:["Earlier writers blamed Parliament or bad ministers. Paine attacked monarchy itself. Why was that a more useful argument in 1776?","Explain two ways the price and the plain language mattered as much as the content.","Six months after the pamphlet, Congress adopted the Declaration. What does that sequence suggest about the relationship between public opinion and the decision?"],key:["Because as long as the fault lay with Parliament or with particular ministers, the remedy was repeal and reconciliation under the same king. Attacking monarchy itself removed reconciliation as an option and made independence the logical conclusion rather than a last resort. It changed the question from how to fix the relationship to why have one. Partial: noting the attack on the king with nothing said about reconciliation being removed is approaching. (HSS-8.1)","Any two: a cheap pamphlet in plain English reached artisans, farmers and sailors rather than only lawyers and merchants, which enormously widened who felt entitled to an opinion; it could be read aloud in a tavern to people who could not read at all; and it signaled that the argument belonged to ordinary people, which is itself part of the case for self government. Partial: one effect named is approaching. (HSS-8.1)","That the delegates were following opinion at least as much as leading it. Many of them had been unwilling to say independence out loud in 1775, and a pamphlet that made the case to the whole public gave them cover to act. A strong answer avoids claiming the pamphlet caused the Declaration and argues instead that it made the decision politically possible. Partial: claiming the pamphlet caused the Declaration overstates it and is approaching. (HSS-8.1)"]},{std:["HSS-8.2"],n:3,dur:100,dir:"Read the note. Answer out loud.",desc:"analyzing McCulloch v. Maryland as a ruling on implied powers and federal supremacy",html:()=>`<p class="big">Congress chartered a second national bank in 1816. Maryland, which disliked it, taxed the bank's Baltimore branch heavily. The cashier, James McCulloch, refused to pay. In 1819 the Supreme Court decided two questions. First, could Congress charter a bank at all, when the Constitution nowhere says so? Chief Justice Marshall wrote that the necessary and proper clause lets Congress choose the means of carrying out powers it does have, and that a bank is a reasonable means of taxing, borrowing and paying debts. Second, could a state tax it? No, Marshall wrote, because the power to tax is the power to destroy, and a state may not destroy what the whole nation has created.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("books",74)}${ico("coin",74)}${ico("flag",74)}</div>`,qs:["State the two holdings in your own words, and name the constitutional provision behind each.","Maryland argued that the states created the national government and so may tax its instruments. Explain what is wrong with that argument according to the decision.","This case is cited more than almost any other in arguments about the size of the federal government. Why?"],key:["First, Congress may charter a bank: the power is implied, resting on the necessary and proper clause as a means of carrying out the enumerated powers to tax, borrow, coin money and pay debts. Second, a state may not tax that bank: the supremacy clause in Article VI makes national law superior, so a state cannot burden a legitimate federal instrument. Partial: one of the two holdings given is approaching. (HSS-8.2)","The decision holds that the Constitution was ratified by the people in conventions, not by the state governments, so the national government's authority runs directly from the people and does not sit at the states' sufferance. And since a tax can be set high enough to destroy, allowing it would let one state nullify a national decision for everyone. Partial: one of the two reasons given is approaching. (HSS-8.2)","Because it settled that the enumerated powers come with a wide choice of means, which is the doorway through which almost every later expansion of federal authority has passed, from the national bank to highways to regulating the economy. Anyone arguing for a broad federal power cites it, and anyone arguing against has to answer it. Partial: naming the case with no later use of it named is approaching. (HSS-8.2)"]},{std:["HSS-8.3"],n:3,dur:90,dir:"Look at the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"analyzing how a president is actually chosen and what that means for a voter",html:()=>`<p class="big">A voter marks one ballot and it does several different things.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Office</th><th>Who counts the vote</th><th>How the winner is decided</th></tr>
     <tr><td>mayor</td><td>the county or city</td><td>most votes in the city</td></tr>
     <tr><td>member of Congress</td><td>the state</td><td>most votes in the district</td></tr>
     <tr><td>United States senator</td><td>the state</td><td>most votes in the whole state</td></tr>
     <tr><td>president</td><td>the state, which then sends electors</td><td>a majority of 538 electoral votes; nearly every state gives all of its electors to its own winner</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("flag",74)}${ico("hands",74)}${ico("star",74)}</div>`,qs:["Explain how a candidate can win the most votes nationally and still lose the presidency.","Senators were chosen by state legislatures until 1913. What did the Seventeenth Amendment change about who a senator answers to?","Three of these four offices are decided by whoever gets the most votes in one place. Why would the framers have made the presidency the exception?"],key:["Because the national total is not what is counted. Each state awards its electors, nearly always all of them to its own winner, so winning a state by one vote and losing another by a million produce the same electoral result. Votes beyond a majority in a state are wasted, and a candidate who piles up large margins in a few states can lead nationally and still fall short of 270. Partial: naming the electoral college with nothing said about votes beyond a majority in a state being wasted is approaching. (HSS-8.3)","It moved the choice from the legislature to the voters, so a senator now answers directly to the state's electorate rather than to the party leaders and legislators who had appointed him. It was intended to break the hold of state machines and corporate influence over Senate seats. Partial: naming the change with nothing said about what it was meant to break is approaching. (HSS-8.3)","Because the presidency is the one office filled by the whole country, and the framers wanted the states to be parties to the choice rather than have it settled by a national popular majority in which small states would count for little. They also distrusted direct election of an executive and expected electors to exercise judgment, which parties quickly ended. Partial: one reason named is approaching. (HSS-8.3)"]}]},{id:21,title:"Who Was Welcome",short:"New Nation",parts:[{std:["HSS-8.4"],n:3,dur:95,dir:"Look at the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"analyzing who the new nation admitted to citizenship and on what terms",html:()=>`<p class="big">The first Congress wrote the rules for becoming an American.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Law</th><th>Who could naturalize</th><th>After how long</th></tr>
     <tr><td>Naturalization Act of 1790</td><td>any free white person of good character</td><td>two years' residence</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Naturalization Act of 1795</td><td>the same</td><td>five years</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Naturalization Act of 1798</td><td>the same</td><td>fourteen years</td></tr>
     <tr><td>1802</td><td>the same</td><td>back to five years</td></tr></table>
     <p class="big">No religious test was imposed, which was unusual: Catholics and Jews could naturalize when several European states still barred them from office or from residence. The word white excluded free Black immigrants and, later, Asian immigrants, and it stood in the statute until 1952.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("sailboat",74)}${ico("books",74)}${ico("star",74)}</div>`,qs:["Compare the two rules in the table: a term of residence that Congress kept changing, and a racial limit it never touched. What does that contrast tell you?","Why would the residence requirement jump to fourteen years in 1798 and drop back to five in 1802?","The law set no religious test at a time when most European states did. Say what that reflects about the new nation's ideals, and what it did not reach."],key:["That the ideal of welcome was real and bounded. The waiting period was an ordinary political question, adjusted whenever a party calculated that new voters would help or hurt it. The racial limit was not treated as a question at all: it survived every change of party for 162 years. What a country argues about and what it assumes are different things. Partial: noting the changes to the waiting period with nothing said about the racial limit never being argued over is approaching. (HSS-8.4)","Because immigrants were voting for the opposition. The Federalists, who passed the 1798 act alongside the Alien and Sedition Acts, calculated that a fourteen year wait would keep new arrivals out of the electorate; when Jefferson's party won in 1800 it promptly restored five years. It is a plain case of citizenship rules being written for partisan advantage. Partial: noting the change with nothing said about partisan advantage is approaching. (HSS-8.4)","It reflects the conviction that belief is not the government's business, which the founders had written into Article VI and the First Amendment and then applied to immigration as well. What it did not reach was race: the same Congress that would not ask a newcomer's faith wrote a color line into who could become an American at all. Partial: naming the absence of a religious test with nothing said about what it did not reach is approaching. (HSS-8.4)"]},{std:["HSS-8.5"],n:3,dur:90,dir:"Read the note. Answer out loud.",desc:"analyzing the Barbary conflicts and the decision to build a navy",html:()=>`<p class="big">For centuries the states of Algiers, Tunis, Tripoli and Morocco had taken tribute from European powers in exchange for leaving their merchant ships alone. Before independence American ships had been covered by British payments; afterward they were not, and American vessels were seized and their crews held for ransom. The United States paid. By the late 1790s the payments ran to roughly a fifth of federal revenue. Jefferson had argued since the 1780s that a navy would cost less than the tribute. In 1801 Tripoli demanded more and he refused, sent the new squadron, and fought a four year naval war that ended with a treaty and no further tribute to Tripoli.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("sailboat",74)}${ico("anchor",74)}${ico("coin",74)}</div>`,qs:["Set out the argument for paying tribute and the argument for building a navy, as someone in 1795 would have made them.","Jefferson generally wanted a small government and low spending, and he built and used a navy. Is that a contradiction? Defend your answer.","What interest was the United States actually protecting here?"],key:["For paying: it is cheaper in the short run than a fleet, it is what every European power did, the country was deep in debt, and a war across the Atlantic with no navy was unwinnable. For building: tribute rises whenever the other side chooses, it buys no permanent security, a fifth of federal revenue is more than ships would cost, and a navy also protects trade everywhere else. Partial: one side of the case given is approaching. (HSS-8.5)","Any defended answer. Not a contradiction: he thought a navy was the cheaper option and that protecting commerce is a core duty even of a small government. A contradiction: he expanded the military and the executive's reach in a way he had criticized in others, and he waged a four year conflict without a declaration of war. A good answer argues with the evidence rather than only asserting. Partial: a verdict asserted with no evidence is approaching. (HSS-8.5)","Its merchant shipping, which carried the trade that federal revenue was almost entirely built on. Seized ships, ransomed crews and insurance costs fell directly on the economy the government depended on, so freedom of the seas was not an abstraction but the country's main source of income. Partial: naming the shipping with nothing said about federal revenue resting on it is approaching. (HSS-8.5)"]},{std:["HSS-8.6"],n:3,dur:95,dir:"Look at the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"analyzing the public services Northeastern cities built as they grew",html:()=>`<p class="big">Cities grew faster than their basic services, and then built them.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Service</th><th>Before</th><th>After</th></tr>
     <tr><td>water</td><td>wells and cisterns in the yard, often next to a privy</td><td>New York's Croton aqueduct, 1842, bringing water 41 miles by gravity</td></tr>
     <tr><td>fire</td><td>competing volunteer companies, sometimes fighting each other over a hydrant</td><td>paid municipal departments from the 1850s</td></tr>
     <tr><td>sewers</td><td>street gutters and cesspools</td><td>built sewer systems from the 1850s onward</td></tr>
     <tr><td>policing</td><td>night watchmen</td><td>uniformed full time forces from the 1840s and 1850s</td></tr></table>
     <p class="big">Boston's death rate rose through the 1840s as its population grew. Cholera outbreaks in 1832 and 1849 killed thousands in Eastern cities.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("drop",74)}${ico("bridge",74)}${ico("hands",74)}</div>`,qs:["Explain why a well in the yard works in a village and fails in a city of 300,000.","Each of these four services moved from volunteers and private arrangements to something the city ran and taxed for. Why would growth force that change?","These projects cost enormous sums and cities built them anyway. Who had to be persuaded, and what argument would have worked?"],key:["Because density concentrates waste. In a village the ground between a privy and a well absorbs and filters what little there is, while in a crowded city thousands of cesspools and privies sit yards from the wells, and groundwater carries contamination between them. That is why piped water from a distant clean source was the only real answer, and why cholera spread through these cities. Partial: naming the crowding with no account of wells and cesspools sitting together is approaching. (HSS-8.6)","Because at scale these services only work if they cover everyone. A fire or an epidemic in a poor block reaches the rich block, competing volunteer companies have an incentive to protect their own subscribers rather than the city, and sewers and aqueducts are useless unless built as one connected system. Problems that cross property lines need an authority that crosses them too. Partial: saying the city was bigger with nothing said about problems crossing property lines is approaching. (HSS-8.6)","Property owners and taxpayers, since they paid. The winning argument was self interest rather than charity: fire and disease do not respect neighborhoods, insurance rates and property values depend on water and fire protection, and a city that cannot keep its workers healthy cannot run its mills and docks. Appeals to public health alone usually lost until an epidemic made the case. Partial: naming the group with nothing said about self interest being the winning argument is approaching. (HSS-8.6)"]}]},{id:22,title:"Books, Camps and Claims",short:"Sections",parts:[{std:["HSS-8.7"],n:3,dur:95,dir:"Look at the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"comparing schooling and literacy in the South with the free states",html:()=>`<p class="big">Two sections, around 1850.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th></th><th>Free states</th><th>Slave states</th></tr>
     <tr><td>White adults who could not read</td><td>about 3 in 100</td><td>about 20 in 100</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Public schools</td><td>common schools in most states, many of them required by law</td><td>few, and mostly in the cities</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Teaching enslaved people to read</td><td>not applicable</td><td>a crime in most states</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Who paid for schooling</td><td>towns, through taxes</td><td>families, through private tutors and academies</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("books",74)}${ico("wheat",74)}${ico("pencil",74)}</div>`,qs:["Why would a region run by wealthy planters not build public schools?","Row three is a law about one group and row one is a number about another. Explain how they are connected.","Name one long term consequence of this difference for the South."],key:["Because the people with the political power already educated their own children privately and would have been taxed to educate other people's. A plantation economy also needed field labor rather than literate workers, and a dispersed rural population made schools expensive per child. Public schooling tends to be built where a broad middle of the population expects to be included, and the South's politics did not rest on that middle. Partial: naming the dispersed population with nothing said about who held the political power is approaching. (HSS-8.7)","They are two sides of one choice. A society that makes teaching one group illegal is a society that does not treat literacy as a general good, and the same politics that banned it for enslaved people declined to fund it for poor white children. Suppressing reading for some made it easier to neglect for everyone outside the planter class. Partial: treating the two facts as unrelated is the misreading to watch for and is approaching. (HSS-8.7)","Any supported consequence: a workforce with less schooling as the country industrialized; fewer engineers, mechanics and teachers at home; slower growth of cities and manufacturing; and after the war, a region that had to build public school systems from almost nothing while being the poorest part of the country. Partial: a consequence asserted with no support is approaching. (HSS-8.7)"]},{std:["HSS-8.8"],n:3,dur:90,dir:"Look at the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"analyzing authority and power in western mining and cattle towns",html:()=>`<p class="big">A mining camp appears in a month and may be gone in three years.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Question</th><th>How a camp answered it</th></tr>
     <tr><td>Who owns a claim?</td><td>miners' meetings wrote their own codes: a claim held only if you worked it</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Who settles a dispute?</td><td>an elected camp committee, or later a territorial court weeks away</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Who may hold a claim at all?</td><td>several camps and states barred or taxed Chinese, Mexican and Native miners</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Who gets rich?</td><td>after the surface gold, companies with capital for machinery</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("mountain",74)}${ico("tent",74)}${ico("gem",74)}</div>`,qs:["Miners wrote their own property codes before any government arrived. What does that tell you about where law comes from?","Row three says camps restricted who could hold a claim. Who benefited, and how was it enforced in a place with no courts?","Explain how the shift described in row four changed who lived in a mining town and what they did."],key:["That people who need a rule will make one, and that a workable rule reflects what the community can actually enforce: a claim held only by working it suited a place with no registry and no sheriff. It also shows the limits of that, since camp codes protected only the people the camp recognized. California later wrote much of this mining law into its statutes. Partial: praising the camp codes with nothing said about who they left out is approaching. (HSS-8.8)","White American miners benefited, by removing competitors from the best ground. With no courts it was enforced by the camp meeting itself and by force, and then formalized in state law such as California's foreign miners' tax. It is a clear case of self government by a community being used against the people that community excluded. Partial: naming who benefited with nothing said about how it was enforced is approaching. (HSS-8.8)","An independent prospector working a pan becomes a wage employee working for a company that owns the equipment, the water rights and the mill. The town shifts from a camp of self employed gamblers to a company town with a payroll, a store and a boss, and the money leaves for distant shareholders. The same change happened in cattle country as big outfits replaced small operators. Partial: naming the companies with nothing said about what changed for the worker or the town is approaching. (HSS-8.8)"]},{std:["HSS-8.9"],n:3,dur:95,dir:"Read the note. Answer out loud.",desc:"analyzing Uncle Tom's Cabin and the effect of a widely read book on the slavery argument",html:()=>`<p class="big">Harriet Beecher Stowe wrote Uncle Tom's Cabin after the Fugitive Slave Act of 1850 required Northerners to help capture people who had escaped. She came from a family of Congregational ministers and built the book on a religious argument: that slavery destroyed families, that no Christian could own a person, and that Northerners who obeyed the new law shared the guilt. It sold about 300,000 copies in its first year in the United States and more than a million in Britain, and was turned into stage plays seen by far more people than read it. Southern writers answered with novels of their own defending plantation life. Frederick Douglass praised its effect and some Black abolitionists criticized its title character as too meek a model.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("books",74)}${ico("heart",74)}${ico("lantern",74)}</div>`,qs:["Abolitionists had been publishing facts and arguments for twenty years. Why would a novel reach people the pamphlets had not?","Stowe aimed much of the book at Northern readers who were not slaveholders. What was she accusing them of, and why was that effective after 1850?","Some Black abolitionists admired the book's effect and criticized its main character. Explain both reactions."],key:["Because a story puts a reader inside a particular family's situation rather than presenting a general wrong, and the thing most Northerners had not imagined was what the sale of a child feels like from inside the household. It also reached people who would never attend a lecture, especially women and, through the stage versions, people who could not read. Facts persuade, narrative implicates. Partial: saying it was popular with no account of what a narrative does that facts do not is approaching. (HSS-8.9)","Of complicity. The 1850 law made Northerners legally obliged to help capture people who had escaped, so she argued that a person who went along was no longer a bystander, and she framed that in the religious terms her readers used about their own conduct. It was effective because the law had just made the accusation literally true in a way it had not been before. Partial: naming the accusation with no link to the 1850 law is approaching. (HSS-8.9)","They admired it because it moved an enormous white audience toward antislavery politics in a way nothing else had, which is a practical gain. They criticized the central character because his patience and acceptance of suffering could be read as the model of a good enslaved man, which cut against the argument that Black Americans should claim their freedom and act for themselves, as Douglass and Walker insisted. Partial: one of the admiration and the criticism given is approaching. (HSS-8.9)"]}]},{id:23,title:"Voted On, Left Unfinished",short:"War and After",parts:[{std:["HSS-8.10"],n:3,dur:95,dir:"Read the note. Answer out loud.",desc:"analyzing the election of 1864 as a decision by voters about continuing the war",html:()=>`<p class="big">The United States held a presidential election in the middle of the war, which no country at war had done. In August 1864 Lincoln expected to lose and wrote a private memorandum saying so. His opponent was General George McClellan, running on a Democratic platform that called the war a failure and demanded immediate negotiations, though McClellan himself repudiated that plank. Then Atlanta fell in September and the military picture changed. Soldiers voted in the field or by absentee ballot where their states allowed it, and they went about four to one for Lincoln. He won with roughly 55 percent of the popular vote and 212 electoral votes to 21.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("flag",74)}${ico("star",74)}${ico("books",74)}</div>`,qs:["Holding the election at all carried an obvious risk. Why hold it?","Soldiers had the hardest war and voted most heavily to continue it. How would you explain that?","What would a negotiated peace in early 1865 most likely have meant for the four million people the Emancipation Proclamation had declared free?"],key:["Because cancelling it would have conceded the Confederacy's central claim, that the United States could not govern itself under strain. Lincoln said that if the election could be postponed the rebellion had already won. Holding it, and losing it, would still have proved the system worked, which is the thing the war was ostensibly about. Partial: noting he might lose with nothing said about what holding the election proved is approaching. (HSS-8.10)","Any reasoned explanation: soldiers had paid for the ground gained and did not want it traded away at a conference table; many had come to support emancipation after serving alongside freedpeople and seeing slavery close up; and a negotiated peace would have made their losses pointless. A strong answer notices that the people with the most direct knowledge of the war were the most committed to finishing it. Partial: an explanation asserted with no reasoning is approaching. (HSS-8.10)","Almost certainly a return to slavery for most of them, or at best an unsettled legal status. The Proclamation was a war measure resting on the president's authority as commander in chief, and the Thirteenth Amendment had not yet been ratified, so a peace that recognized the Confederacy or restored the old Union without an amendment would have left emancipation without legal foundation. Partial: naming the risk with nothing said about the Proclamation being a war measure is approaching. (HSS-8.10)"]},{std:["HSS-8.11"],n:4,dur:105,dir:"Look at the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"assessing what Reconstruction changed permanently and what it failed to secure",html:()=>`<p class="big">A reckoning, in two columns.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Changed, and stayed changed</th><th>Attempted, and lost</th></tr>
     <tr><td>slavery abolished by constitutional amendment</td><td>the vote, stripped back by poll taxes and literacy tests within a generation</td></tr>
     <tr><td>citizenship by birth, due process and equal protection written into the Constitution</td><td>land, which was asked for and never provided</td></tr>
     <tr><td>the first public school systems in the Southern states</td><td>equal schools, replaced by segregated and unequally funded ones</td></tr>
     <tr><td>families made legal, and churches, schools and colleges founded and kept</td><td>federal protection, withdrawn in 1877</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("books",74)}${ico("hands",74)}${ico("sprout",74)}</div>`,qs:["Pick one item from the left column and explain why it could not be undone the way the right column items were.","Land is in the right column. Explain why its absence made the other losses more likely.","Was Reconstruction a failure? Take a position and defend it using both columns.","The Fourteenth and Fifteenth Amendments were largely unenforced for sixty years and then became the basis of the civil rights cases of the twentieth century. What does that sequence show about how a constitutional right works?"],key:["Any item with sound reasoning. The amendments are the strongest case: undoing them would require three quarters of the states, so they survived even total political defeat, while voting rules and federal troop deployments could be changed by a legislature or an administration. The institutions freedpeople built and owned are also a good answer, because nobody had to grant them. Partial: an item named with no reasoning about why it lasted is approaching. (HSS-8.11)","Because without land a family had no independent income, nothing to borrow against and no way to survive a year of political retaliation. A sharecropper who registered to vote could be put off the land and out of credit by the man who owned both, so economic dependence made political rights unenforceable in practice. Land was the foundation the rest needed. Partial: naming the land with nothing said about political rights being unenforceable without it is approaching. (HSS-8.11)","Any defended position. Failure: its central promises of equal citizenship, voting and land were defeated within a generation and segregation followed for sixty years. Not a failure: slavery ended permanently, three amendments entered the Constitution, Black southerners built families, schools, colleges and institutions that lasted, and the amendments later became the legal basis for undoing segregation. The strongest answers use both columns rather than one. Partial: a one-sided verdict is approaching; the strongest answers use both columns. (HSS-8.11)","That writing a right down is necessary and not sufficient: it needs someone with power willing to enforce it, and when that will disappears the text alone does very little. It also shows the text is not wasted, because it stays available, and a later generation with the political will found the law already written and did not have to win an amendment first. Partial: saying the text was useless is approaching, since it stayed available for a later generation. (HSS-8.11)"]},{std:["HSS-8.12"],n:3,dur:95,dir:"Look at the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"analyzing who gained and who did not from industrial growth, and the argument about what government should do",html:()=>`<p class="big">The same forty years, from two directions.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>By 1900</th><th></th></tr>
     <tr><td>National wealth</td><td>the United States was the largest industrial producer in the world</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Real wages</td><td>higher than in 1870 for most workers</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Distribution</td><td>roughly 1 percent of families held a large share of the nation's wealth</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Conditions</td><td>sixty hour weeks were normal, industrial injury was common, and about 1.75 million children aged 10 to 15 worked for wages</td></tr></table>
     <p class="big">One side of the argument held that government should leave the economy alone, because interference slows growth and everyone gains from growth. The other held that the market had produced conditions no one would choose and that only law could change hours, safety, child labor and the power of the largest firms.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("coin",74)}${ico("truck",74)}${ico("hands",74)}</div>`,qs:["Rows two and four appear to disagree. Explain how both can be true.","State the strongest version of each side of the argument in the last paragraph, then say which you find more convincing and why.","Name two things that were in fact done about these conditions in the decades that followed, and say who pushed for them."],key:["Because a wage can rise while the conditions attached to it stay harsh. Workers in 1900 could buy more than workers in 1870 and were still working sixty hours in dangerous places, and averages conceal the difference between a skilled machinist and a child in a cannery. Growth and fairness are separate measures, and this period is the standard example of improving on one and not the other. Partial: naming one measure only, growth or fairness, is approaching. (HSS-8.12)","Leave it alone: growth raised real wages, created millions of jobs and made goods cheap, and interference would slow the investment that produced those gains. Act: no individual chose a sixty hour week or sent a ten year old into a mill, those terms came from the inequality of bargaining power, and only law can change what no single worker can refuse. Either position earns full credit if it is argued with evidence from the chart. Partial: a position taken with no evidence from the chart is approaching. (HSS-8.12)","Any two with the right advocates: state and then federal limits on child labor and on hours, pushed by unions, settlement house workers and journalists; workplace safety laws and workers' compensation, pushed after well publicized disasters; antitrust enforcement, pushed by farmers, small businesses and Progressive politicians; and the income tax under the Sixteenth Amendment, pushed by Populists and Progressives. Partial: a reform named with no advocates named is approaching. (HSS-8.12)"]}]},{id:24,title:"The Greenhouse Peas",short:"Heredity",parts:[{std:["LS3"],n:3,dur:90,dir:"Look at the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"tracing a change in a gene through the protein it codes for to the trait that results",html:()=>`<p class="big">The school greenhouse grew four lines of peas for the exhibition. All four carry the same gene, which codes for one enzyme in the path that builds the purple flower pigment.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Line</th><th>Protein the gene codes for</th><th>Flowers</th></tr>
     <tr><td>G1, unchanged</td><td>full length enzyme, full activity</td><td>deep purple</td></tr>
     <tr><td>G2, one base changed, an amino acid in the active site replaced</td><td>enzyme folds correctly and has no activity at all</td><td>white</td></tr>
     <tr><td>G3, one base changed, an amino acid on the outer surface replaced</td><td>enzyme folds correctly and has full activity</td><td>deep purple</td></tr>
     <tr><td>G4, six extra bases in the middle, two extra amino acids</td><td>enzyme folds loosely and has a third of normal activity</td><td>pale lilac</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("purpleFlower",74)}${ico("sprout",74)}</div>`,qs:["G2 and G3 are each a single base change and only one of them changes the flower. Explain the difference exactly.","Given G4's result, say where in the protein its two extra amino acids must be sitting, and how you can tell.","Rank all four lines by pigment and state the one quantity the ranking follows."],key:["G2's replaced amino acid is in the active site, the small pocket where the enzyme actually does its chemistry, so even though the protein folds perfectly it can no longer carry out the step, no pigment is built and the flower is white. G3's replaced amino acid is on the outer surface, where the chain can take a different amino acid without altering the pocket, so the enzyme keeps full activity and the flower stays deep purple. The same size of change gives everything or nothing depending on where in the protein it lands. Partial: saying one change matters and the other does not, with nothing said about G2's amino acid sitting in the active site, is approaching. (LS3)","They must be sitting somewhere that disturbs the folding without destroying the active site, most likely in a loop or a hinge near the working pocket rather than in the pocket itself. We can tell from the result: a third of normal activity means the pocket is still built and still functioning, so the insertion did not land inside it, but it is working poorly, so the insertion is close enough to loosen the shape around it. Had the two amino acids landed in the pocket the flowers would be white like G2, and had they landed far away on the surface they would be deep purple like G3. Partial: placing the insertion near but not in the pocket with no reasoning given from the third of normal activity is approaching. (LS3)","Deepest first: G1 and G3 are both deep purple with a full activity enzyme, then G4 is pale lilac with a third of the activity, then G2 is white with none. The ranking follows the enzyme's activity and nothing else. G1 and G3 have different DNA and different amino acid sequences and give the same flower, because what the flower responds to is how much pigment gets built. Partial: the four lines ranked correctly with the quantity not named as the enzyme's activity is approaching. (LS3)"]}]},{id:25,title:"The Exhibition Fossil Case",short:"Evolution",parts:[{std:["LS4"],n:3,dur:90,dir:"Study both charts. Answer out loud.",desc:"reading a fossil sequence and a graded protein comparison as evidence of common ancestry",html:()=>`<p class="big">The case holds a column collected on the school's quarry trip. Layers pile up newest on top.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Layer</th><th>Rock</th><th>Fossils in it</th></tr>
     <tr><td>1, 0 to 2 m</td><td>soil</td><td>oak leaves and snail shells like those alive today</td></tr>
     <tr><td>2, 2 to 9 m</td><td>silt</td><td>a small horse with one toe, and a pig like animal</td></tr>
     <tr><td>3, 9 to 21 m</td><td>chalk</td><td>coiled shelled ammonites, and no mammal of any kind</td></tr>
     <tr><td>4, 21 to 35 m</td><td>sandstone</td><td>trilobites and brachiopods, and no fish with jaws</td></tr></table>
     <p class="big">One blood protein, 146 amino acids long, compared with the human version. The chart counts how many of the 146 differ.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Animal</th><th>Amino acids different, out of 146</th></tr>
     <tr><td>chimpanzee</td><td>0</td></tr>
     <tr><td>gorilla</td><td>1</td></tr>
     <tr><td>rhesus monkey</td><td>8</td></tr>
     <tr><td>dog</td><td>15</td></tr>
     <tr><td>chicken</td><td>45</td></tr>
     <tr><td>frog</td><td>67</td></tr>
     <tr><td>lamprey</td><td>125</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("globe",74)}${ico("books",74)}${ico("mountain",74)}</div>`,qs:["Read the fossil case from oldest to youngest and describe the sequence of what appears and what is still absent.","Describe the pattern in the protein chart, and say how it compares with the order the fossil case gives.","A visitor says the protein numbers could be coincidence. Say what would have to be true of the numbers for that to hold, and whether the chart shows it."],key:["Oldest first: layer 4, trilobites and brachiopods with no jawed fish anywhere in it; layer 3, chalk with ammonites and no mammals at all; layer 2, silt with a one toed horse and a pig like animal; layer 1, the living forms. Shelled sea animals come first, then animals with jaws and backbones higher up, then mammals only in the upper layers, and at every level the forms that appear later are absent below. The record is ordered, and the order is the same wherever the same layers are found. Partial: the four layers in the right order with nothing said about the later forms being absent from the layers below is approaching. (LS4)","The differences grow steadily with how distantly related the animal is usually taken to be: 0 for a chimpanzee, 1 for a gorilla, 8 for a monkey, 15 for a dog, 45 for a chicken, 67 for a frog, 125 for a lamprey. The fossil case puts jawless and shelled forms lowest, then jawed and four limbed forms, then mammals near the top. The two lines of evidence agree: the animals whose groups appear lowest and earliest in the rock are the ones whose protein differs most from ours, and the animals from the uppermost layers differ least. Partial: describing the rising counts with no comparison drawn against the order the fossil case gives is approaching. (LS4)","For coincidence to hold, the 146 positions would have to differ in a way unrelated to anything else, so the counts would come out in no particular order against the fossil record or the anatomy, and a frog might come out closer to us than a monkey. The chart does not show that. The counts fall in a single graded order that matches the fossil sequence and matches the anatomical groupings as well, and one protein's seven numbers lining up with two independent bodies of evidence is not what chance produces. The way to test it further is to repeat the comparison with other proteins and see whether the same order appears, which is what has been done. Partial: answering that the order is not coincidence with nothing said about what the counts would look like if it were is approaching. (LS4)"]},{std:["LS4"],n:3,dur:90,dir:"Study both sets of numbers. Work on your paper and answer out loud.",desc:"explaining natural selection from a trait distribution and distinguishing it from artificial selection",html:()=>`<p class="big">A class has scored the dandelions in the school lawn for nine years. The lawn is mown every week at 5 cm. One hundred and sixty plants were scored each year.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Height of the flower stalk</th><th>Year 1</th><th>Year 4</th><th>Year 9</th></tr>
     <tr><td>under 5 cm</td><td>22</td><td>61</td><td>112</td></tr>
     <tr><td>5 to 12 cm</td><td>59</td><td>68</td><td>41</td></tr>
     <tr><td>over 12 cm</td><td>79</td><td>31</td><td>7</td></tr></table>
     <p class="big">The exhibition's corn display is a different story. A wild grass carrying eight small hard seeds on a spike became a cob of six hundred soft seeds over about nine thousand years of farmers choosing which seed to plant.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("purpleFlower",74)}${ico("corn",74)}${ico("sprout",74)}</div>`,qs:["Explain the shift in the lawn. Name the trait that varies, what is doing the selecting, and what has to be inherited.","Work out the shares over 12 cm in year 1 and in year 9, then say what the chart predicts for an unmown corner of the same lawn.","Compare the lawn with the corn display, and say why the corn changed so much more even though the lawn was under pressure every single week."],key:["Stalk height already varies among the dandelions and is inherited. A mower set at 5 cm cuts the head off any plant whose stalk is taller than that, so tall stalked plants lose their flowers before they can set seed while short stalked plants flower and seed below the blade. The next year's plants come mostly from the short stalked parents, so the share under 5 cm climbs from 22 of 160 to 112. The mower does not shorten any plant permanently; it decides which plants leave seed. Partial: two of the three steps given is approaching, and the inheritance step is usually the one left out. (LS4)","Year 1: 79 of 160, which is about 49 percent. Year 9: 7 of 160, which is about 4 percent. In an unmown corner there is no blade removing tall stalks, and a tall stalk carries seed further on the wind, so the selection would run the other way and the tall end would be favored. The chart predicts the unmown corner would hold a far larger share of plants over 12 cm, which is what unmown verges actually look like. Partial: working out the 49 and 4 percent with no prediction made for the unmown corner is approaching. (LS4)","In the lawn nothing chooses: a mower set for a different purpose happens to decide which dandelions seed, which is natural selection. With the corn, farmers chose the seed of the plants they wanted every single year, which is artificial selection, and they kept choosing for nine thousand generations. The lawn has had nine generations of moderate pressure on one trait. The corn had thousands of generations of extreme pressure on several traits at once, and each tiny gain was kept and built on, which is how eight hard seeds became six hundred soft ones. Time and the strength of the selection are both part of the answer. Partial: naming the selecting agent in both cases with nothing said about the number of generations and the strength of the selection is approaching. (LS4)"]}]},{id:26,title:"The Eclipse Board",short:"Universe",parts:[{std:["MS-ESS1"],n:3,dur:85,dir:"Look at the board. Answer out loud.",desc:"explaining eclipses and the lunar cycle and naming the force that holds the orbit",html:()=>`<p class="big">One of the exhibition boards lists the eclipses visible from the town over four years, with the moon's appearance on each date.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>When</th><th>Kind of eclipse</th><th>How the moon looked that night</th></tr>
     <tr><td>March, year 1</td><td>lunar, partial</td><td>fully lit</td></tr>
     <tr><td>April, year 1</td><td>solar, total</td><td>not visible at all</td></tr>
     <tr><td>September, year 1</td><td>lunar, partial</td><td>fully lit</td></tr>
     <tr><td>October, year 1</td><td>solar, annular</td><td>not visible at all</td></tr>
     <tr><td>March, year 2</td><td>lunar, total</td><td>fully lit</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("moon",74)}${ico("sun",74)}${ico("telescope",74)}</div>`,qs:["Every lunar eclipse on the board falls on a fully lit moon and every solar eclipse on an invisible one. Explain both with the positions of the sun, Earth and the moon.","There are twelve or thirteen fully lit moons in a year and only a handful of lunar eclipses. Explain the shortfall.","Name the force that holds the moon in the orbit this pattern depends on, and say what two quantities set its strength."],key:["A lunar eclipse is Earth's shadow falling on the moon, which can only happen when the moon is on the opposite side of Earth from the sun, and that is exactly the arrangement that turns the moon's whole lit face toward us and makes it look fully lit. A solar eclipse is the moon's shadow falling on Earth, which can only happen when the moon is between Earth and the sun, and that is the arrangement that turns the moon's lit face away from us and leaves it invisible. The phase is not a coincidence; it is the same geometry read from the ground. Partial: accounting for one of the two kinds of eclipse by its arrangement is approaching. (MS-ESS1)","The moon's orbit is tilted about five degrees to the plane of Earth's orbit, so most of the time the fully lit moon passes above or below Earth's shadow rather than through it. An eclipse needs the moon to be fully lit and crossing the plane of Earth's orbit at the same time, and those two cycles have different lengths, so they only coincide a few times a year. That is also why the board shows eclipses clustering in pairs a couple of weeks apart. Partial: naming the tilted orbit with nothing said about the two cycles having to coincide is approaching. (MS-ESS1)","Gravity. Its strength is set by the masses of the two bodies and the distance between them: it grows in proportion to each mass and falls off with the square of the distance. Earth's mass and the 384,000 km to the moon are what fix the pull that bends the moon's motion into an orbit instead of a straight line, and the same force with the sun's mass and 150 million km holds Earth on its own path. Partial: naming gravity with only one of mass and distance given is approaching. (MS-ESS1)"]},{std:["MS-ESS1"],n:3,dur:85,dir:"Use both charts. Work on your paper and tell your teacher each answer.",desc:"reading a scale model of the solar system and using a dated ash bed to match distant layers",html:()=>`<p class="big">The exhibition's model is built to one meter for every ten million kilometers.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Planet</th><th>Real distance from the sun</th><th>On the model</th></tr>
     <tr><td>Mercury</td><td>58 million km</td><td>5.8 m</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Earth</td><td>150 million km</td><td>15.0 m</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Jupiter</td><td>778 million km</td><td>77.8 m</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Neptune</td><td>4,500 million km</td><td>450 m</td></tr></table>
     <p class="big">The exhibition's dated column, with a bed of volcanic ash inside the silt.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Unit, top to bottom</th><th>Age of the rock</th></tr>
     <tr><td>soil</td><td>7,000 years</td></tr>
     <tr><td>silt, 25 million years old at its base</td><td>25 million years</td></tr>
     <tr><td>a bed of volcanic ash inside the silt</td><td>21 million years</td></tr>
     <tr><td>chalk</td><td>85 million years</td></tr>
     <tr><td>sandstone</td><td>470 million years</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("globe",74)}${ico("ruler",74)}${ico("volcano",74)}</div>`,qs:["The school hall is 40 m long. Say which of the four planets fit inside it and what that shows about the solar system.","On this model Earth would be 1.3 mm across. Work out the sun's size on the model and say why the exhibition puts sizes and distances on two separate boards.","The same ash bed, with the same 21 million year date, is found inside the silt at a quarry 300 km away. Say what that lets the exhibition claim about the two places, and name one thing it does not establish."],key:["Mercury at 5.8 m and Earth at 15.0 m fit inside the hall, and Jupiter at 77.8 m and Neptune at 450 m do not. Two of the eight planets fit in the hall and the rest need the length of the playing fields and beyond, which shows the solar system is mostly outer solar system: the four inner planets are crowded into the first stretch and the gaps grow enormously further out. Partial: naming the two planets that fit with nothing said about the gaps growing enormously further out is approaching. (MS-ESS1)","The scale is 1 m for every 10 million km, so 1 mm stands for 10,000 km. The sun is 1,392,000 km across, which is 139.2 mm, about 13.9 cm. So the sun is a ball the size of a melon, Earth is a grain of sand 15 m away, and Neptune's grain is 450 m away. A single board cannot show both: at a scale where the sun is visible, Earth is a speck, and at a scale where Earth is visible, the distances run off the building. Two boards is the honest way to show it. Partial: working out the 13.9 cm sun with nothing said about why one board cannot carry both scales is approaching. (MS-ESS1)","It lets the exhibition say the silt at both quarries was being laid down at the same time, because an ash bed is laid down in days or weeks by a single eruption and then buried, so it marks one moment across every place the ash reached. That is how layers are matched between sites too far apart to walk a bed across. It does not establish that the two places had the same climate, or the same depth of water, or the same animals and plants, or that the silt above and below the ash is the same age at the two quarries. Same moment at the ash is all the ash gives. Partial: saying the two silts are the same age with nothing named that the ash bed does not establish is approaching. (MS-ESS1)"]}]},{id:27,title:"Sand and Gravel",short:"Human Impact",parts:[{std:["MS-ESS3"],n:3,dur:95,dir:"Look at the chart. Work on your paper and answer out loud.",desc:"separating population growth from per person consumption in total demand on a resource",html:()=>`<p class="big">Sand and gravel used in the county, from the exhibition's own research board. The permitted pits hold about 24 million tons still to be dug.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Year</th><th>People</th><th>Tons per person per year</th><th>Total tons</th></tr>
     <tr><td>1970</td><td>60,000</td><td>5.0</td><td>300,000</td></tr>
     <tr><td>1990</td><td>95,000</td><td>6.4</td><td>608,000</td></tr>
     <tr><td>2010</td><td>140,000</td><td>7.1</td><td>994,000</td></tr>
     <tr><td>2025</td><td>175,000</td><td>6.0</td><td>1,050,000</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("truck",74)}${ico("mountain",74)}${ico("box",74)}</div>`,qs:["The total rose by a factor of 3.5 from 1970 to 2025. Work out how much of that came from each of the two causes.","Work out how many years the permitted pits last at the 2025 rate, and again if the county reaches 250,000 people at the same per person rate.","Name two effects on Earth's systems of digging at that rate, and say what the chart suggests about recycling old concrete as a way of stretching the pits."],key:["People rose from 60,000 to 175,000, a factor of about 2.92. Per person use rose from 5.0 to 6.0 tons, a factor of 1.2. Multiplied together, 2.92 times 1.2 is about 3.5, and 300,000 times 3.5 is about 1,050,000, which matches. Population is much the larger cause, about three times over, with per person use adding a further fifth on top. Partial: naming both factors with only one of the two multipliers worked out is approaching. (MS-ESS3)","At 1,050,000 tons a year, 24,000,000 divided by 1,050,000 is about 22.9 years, so just under 23 years. At 250,000 people and 6.0 tons each the yearly total would be 1,500,000 tons, and 24,000,000 divided by 1,500,000 is 16 years. Growing from 175,000 to 250,000 people cuts the county's remaining supply by about seven years without anyone using more each. Partial: one of the two figures worked out is approaching. (MS-ESS3)","Any two: a pit removes the soil, the plants and the animals above it and leaves a hole that will never be what it was; digging below the water table changes how groundwater moves and can draw down nearby wells; river sand taken faster than the river replaces it deepens the channel and undercuts banks and bridges; and the hauling itself means heavy traffic, dust and fuel burned on every ton. The chart suggests recycling works on the per person column, which has moved only from 5.0 to 6.0 and back, while the population column has nearly trebled. So recycling can stretch the pits, but on these numbers it would have to cut per person use by well over a third to buy back the years that growth to 250,000 people would take away. Partial: two effects named with nothing said about recycling acting on the smaller of the two columns is approaching. (MS-ESS3)"]}]},{id:28,title:"The Physics Booth",short:"Forces",parts:[{std:["MS-PS2"],n:3,dur:85,dir:"Use both charts. Work on your paper and answer out loud.",desc:"applying the third law to a push between two objects and relating force and mass to motion",html:()=>`<p class="big">The booth has two carts on a long smooth track with a squeezed spring between them. Visitors release the spring.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Cart</th><th>Mass</th><th>Speed just after release</th></tr>
     <tr><td>cart with the weight pack</td><td>1.2 kg</td><td>0.25 m/s</td></tr>
     <tr><td>plain cart</td><td>0.3 kg</td><td>1.00 m/s</td></tr></table>
     <p class="big">A cart is then pulled along the track with a measured force.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Trial</th><th>Force</th><th>Mass</th><th>Acceleration</th></tr>
     <tr><td>1</td><td>1.2 N</td><td>0.3 kg</td><td>4 m/s each second</td></tr>
     <tr><td>2</td><td>1.2 N</td><td>1.2 kg</td><td>1 m/s each second</td></tr>
     <tr><td>3</td><td>4.8 N</td><td>0.3 kg</td><td>16 m/s each second</td></tr>
     <tr><td>4</td><td>3.0 N</td><td>0.6 kg</td><td>5 m/s each second</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("calculator",74)}${ico("box",74)}${ico("trophy",74)}</div>`,qs:["A visitor says the heavier cart must have been pushed harder because the spring had more to move. Answer with Newton's third law.","Multiply mass by speed for each cart. Say what the two products show, then work out the plain cart's speed if the weight pack cart had massed 2.4 kg and still left at 0.25 m/s.","Which two trials differ only in mass and which only in force? Say what each pair shows, then confirm trial 4 with the rule."],key:["The visitor has it backwards. The spring pushes on both carts with forces equal in size and opposite in direction, at the same instant and for the same length of time, and nothing about one cart being heavier changes either force. What the extra mass changes is the result of that force: the same push gives the heavier cart a quarter of the speed. Equal forces, unequal motion, because the masses are unequal. Partial: answering that the two forces are equal with nothing said about the extra mass changing the result rather than the force is approaching. (MS-PS2)","Weight pack cart: 1.2 times 0.25 is 0.30. Plain cart: 0.3 times 1.00 is also 0.30. The quantities of motion are equal in size and opposite in direction, which is what a pair of equal and opposite forces acting for the same time produces. A 2.4 kg cart at 0.25 m/s carries 0.60, so the 0.3 kg cart would leave at 0.60 divided by 0.3, which is 2.00 m/s. Partial: finding both products equal to 0.30 with the 2.00 m/s prediction not worked out is approaching. (MS-PS2)","Trials 1 and 2 differ only in mass, 0.3 kg against 1.2 kg at 1.2 N, and the acceleration falls from 4 to 1, so four times the mass gives a quarter of the acceleration. Trials 1 and 3 differ only in force, 1.2 N against 4.8 N at 0.3 kg, and the acceleration rises from 4 to 16, so four times the force gives four times the acceleration. Trial 4 confirms it: 0.6 kg times 5 m/s each second is 3.0 N. Partial: naming both pairs of trials with trial 4 not checked against force equals mass times acceleration is approaching. (MS-PS2)"]},{std:["MS-PS2"],n:3,dur:85,dir:"Use all three charts. Answer out loud.",desc:"identifying what changes electric and magnetic forces, naming fields, and relating weight to mass",html:()=>`<p class="big">The booth's electromagnet is a nail wrapped in wire, tested on a pile of paperclips.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Trial</th><th>Turns of wire</th><th>Current</th><th>Core</th><th>Paperclips held</th></tr>
     <tr><td>1</td><td>100</td><td>0.6 A</td><td>iron nail</td><td>7</td></tr>
     <tr><td>2</td><td>300</td><td>0.6 A</td><td>iron nail</td><td>21</td></tr>
     <tr><td>3</td><td>100</td><td>1.8 A</td><td>iron nail</td><td>20</td></tr>
     <tr><td>4</td><td>100</td><td>0.6 A</td><td>glass rod</td><td>0</td></tr></table>
     <p class="big">Two charged strips hang facing each other and the force pushing them apart is measured.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Gap</th><th>Each strip rubbed 5 times</th><th>Each strip rubbed 20 times</th></tr>
     <tr><td>1 cm</td><td>1.60 mN</td><td>6.20 mN</td></tr>
     <tr><td>2 cm</td><td>0.40 mN</td><td>1.55 mN</td></tr>
     <tr><td>4 cm</td><td>0.10 mN</td><td>0.39 mN</td></tr></table>
     <p class="big">The same 1 kg exhibition trophy, weighed in three places.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Where</th><th>Weight on the scale</th></tr>
     <tr><td>the hall</td><td>9.8 N</td></tr>
     <tr><td>on the moon</td><td>1.6 N</td></tr>
     <tr><td>at Jupiter's cloud tops</td><td>24.8 N</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("key",74)}${ico("trophy",74)}${ico("globe",74)}</div>`,qs:["Use the first chart. Name the three factors and the pair that shows each, then say which one made the whole magnet useless.","Use the second chart. Describe the pattern down a column and the pattern across a row, with numbers.","Explain all three trophy weights, then name what fills the gap in each of the three experiments."],key:["Turns of wire: trials 1 and 2, where trebling them from 100 to 300 trebles the hold from 7 clips to 21. Current: trials 1 and 3, where trebling it from 0.6 A to 1.8 A takes the hold from 7 clips to 20. The core: trials 1 and 4, where swapping the iron nail for a glass rod drops the hold to nothing at all. The core is what made it useless, because glass is not magnetic and so nothing concentrates the field the current makes, and no amount of extra current or extra turns would make glass behave like iron. Partial: all three factors named with trial pairs but nothing said about why no extra current could rescue a glass core is approaching. (MS-PS2)","Down a column the force falls off with the square of the gap: with 5 rubs it goes 1.60 mN at 1 cm, 0.40 mN at 2 cm and 0.10 mN at 4 cm, so doubling the gap quarters the force each time. Across a row the force rises with the charge: four times the rubbing takes 1.60 mN to 6.20 mN at 1 cm and 0.40 mN to 1.55 mN at 2 cm, close to four times in both. Charge scales the whole set of readings up, while the gap sets the shape of the fall. Partial: describing one of the two patterns with figures is approaching, and the row pattern is usually the one left out. (MS-PS2)","The trophy holds the same 1 kg of matter in all three places. Weight is the gravitational force on it, which grows with the mass of the world and falls off with the square of the distance to that world's center, so the moon's small mass gives 1.6 N, Earth gives 9.8 N and Jupiter's enormous mass gives 24.8 N at its cloud tops. Every gap in the three experiments is filled by a field: a magnetic field between the nail and the clips, an electric field between the two charged strips, and a gravitational field between the trophy and each world. Partial: explaining two of the three weights, or all three with no field named for the three gaps, is approaching. (MS-PS2)"]}]},{id:29,title:"The Energy Table",short:"Energy",parts:[{std:["MS-PS3"],n:4,dur:110,dir:"Use both charts. Work on your paper and tell your teacher each answer.",desc:"relating kinetic energy to mass and speed and stored energy to the arrangement of objects",html:()=>`<p class="big">The booth's cart was timed through a gate and its kinetic energy worked out.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Trial</th><th>Mass</th><th>Speed</th><th>Kinetic energy</th></tr>
     <tr><td>1</td><td>0.5 kg</td><td>2 m/s</td><td>1 J</td></tr>
     <tr><td>2</td><td>2.0 kg</td><td>2 m/s</td><td>4 J</td></tr>
     <tr><td>3</td><td>0.5 kg</td><td>8 m/s</td><td>16 J</td></tr>
     <tr><td>4</td><td>2.0 kg</td><td>4 m/s</td><td>16 J</td></tr></table>
     <p class="big">Stored energy measured for a 2 kg display globe held above the table, and for the rubber band on the booth's catapult.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Arrangement</th><th>Stored energy</th></tr>
     <tr><td>globe 0.5 m above the table</td><td>9.8 J</td></tr>
     <tr><td>globe 1.5 m above the table</td><td>29.4 J</td></tr>
     <tr><td>globe 2.5 m above the table</td><td>49.0 J</td></tr>
     <tr><td>catapult band pulled back 8 cm</td><td>0.64 J</td></tr>
     <tr><td>catapult band pulled back 24 cm</td><td>5.76 J</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("globe",74)}${ico("balloons",74)}</div>`,qs:["Trials 3 and 4 both come to 16 J by different routes. Show how each one gets there from trial 1.","State the rule for mass and the rule for speed from this chart, then work out the energy of a 2.0 kg cart at 8 m/s.","Use the globe rows. State the rule, and explain why a visitor could correctly give the globe on the table a stored energy that is not zero.","The band's energy rises nine times when its pull trebles, while the globe's rises only five times when its height is five times greater. Explain the difference in terms of the force involved."],key:["Trial 3 keeps trial 1's 0.5 kg and multiplies the speed by four, from 2 m/s to 8 m/s, and four squared is sixteen, so 1 J becomes 16 J. Trial 4 multiplies trial 1's mass by four, from 0.5 kg to 2.0 kg, and its speed by two, from 2 m/s to 4 m/s, and 4 times 2 squared is 4 times 4, which is 16, so again 1 J becomes 16 J. Two different routes to the same energy, because mass counts once and speed counts twice. Partial: showing one of the two routes to 16 J is approaching. (MS-PS3)","Mass: trials 1 and 2 multiply the mass by four at the same 2 m/s and the energy goes up four times, 1 J to 4 J, so energy is proportional to mass. Speed: trials 1 and 3 multiply the speed by four at the same 0.5 kg and the energy goes up sixteen times, 1 J to 16 J, so energy goes up with the square of the speed. A 2.0 kg cart at 8 m/s is four times trial 1's mass and four times its speed, so 1 J times 4 times 16, which is 64 J. Partial: both rules stated with the 64 J not worked out, or a figure of 16 J that applies only one of the two changes, is approaching. (MS-PS3)","Stored energy is proportional to the height above the table: 0.5 m gives 9.8 J, three times that height gives three times the energy at 29.4 J, and five times gives 49.0 J. A visitor who measures from the hall floor rather than the table top is measuring the same globe against a different reference position, and since the table is above the floor the globe sitting on the table has a positive stored energy from there. Both answers are right because stored energy belongs to an arrangement of the globe and Earth and only has a value once the reference is named. Partial: stating the proportion to height with nothing said about the hall floor being just as legitimate a reference as the table top is approaching. (MS-PS3)","Earth pulls the globe with practically the same force at every height in the hall, so each extra meter of lift stores the same amount and the total rises in step with the height, five times the height giving five times the energy. The rubber band pulls back harder the further it is drawn, so each extra centimeter stores more than the one before, and the total rises with the square of the pull, three times the pull giving nine times the energy. How the force changes as the parts move is what sets the rule, which is why a rule from one store cannot be read onto another. Partial: noting that the two rules differ with nothing said about the band pulling back harder the further it is drawn is approaching. (MS-PS3)"]}]},{id:30,title:"The Wave Tank",short:"Waves",parts:[{std:["MS-PS4"],n:3,dur:80,dir:"Look at the chart. Work on your paper and answer out loud.",desc:"relating amplitude to the energy in a wave and using wavelength and frequency to find wave speed",html:()=>`<p class="big">The exhibition's wave tank sends waves at a paddle, which records the energy each crest delivers.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Wave</th><th>Amplitude</th><th>Wavelength</th><th>Crests per second</th><th>Energy each crest delivers</th></tr>
     <tr><td>A</td><td>1 cm</td><td>20 cm</td><td>6</td><td>1 unit</td></tr>
     <tr><td>B</td><td>2 cm</td><td>20 cm</td><td>6</td><td>4 units</td></tr>
     <tr><td>C</td><td>5 cm</td><td>20 cm</td><td>6</td><td>25 units</td></tr>
     <tr><td>D</td><td>5 cm</td><td>40 cm</td><td>3</td><td>25 units</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("wave",74)}${ico("drop",74)}</div>`,qs:["Use all of A, B and C to show that the energy follows the square of the amplitude.","Work out the speed of waves C and D and say what the match tells you about what sets a water wave's speed in this tank.","A visitor asks which of the four waves carries the most energy to the paddle in one minute. Work it out and explain your reasoning."],key:["A to B doubles the amplitude from 1 cm to 2 cm, and two squared is four, which is exactly the energy. A to C multiplies it by five, from 1 cm to 5 cm, and five squared is twenty five, which is again exactly the energy. All three rows fit energy going up with the square of the amplitude, which is why amplitude is the measure of the energy in a wave. Partial: showing the square relationship on one of the two comparisons is approaching. (MS-PS4)","Speed is wavelength times crests per second. C: 0.20 m times 6 is 1.2 m per second. D: 0.40 m times 3 is also 1.2 m per second. The two match although their wavelengths and frequencies are different, so the speed is set by the water and the tank rather than by how the waves are made. That is why doubling the wavelength forced the frequency to halve. Partial: working out one of the two speeds, or both with nothing said about the water and the tank setting the speed, is approaching. (MS-PS4)","Wave C. Energy per crest depends only on amplitude, so C and D both deliver 25 units a crest, more than A's 1 and B's 4. But C delivers 6 crests a second and D only 3, so over 60 seconds C brings 6 times 60 times 25, which is 9,000 units, while D brings 3 times 60 times 25, which is 4,500 units. Amplitude decides what one crest carries and frequency decides how many crests arrive, so both are needed for the total over time. Partial: naming C or D as the highest energy per crest with the minute totals not worked out is approaching. (MS-PS4)"]},{std:["MS-PS4"],n:3,dur:80,dir:"Use the chart and the two methods. Answer out loud.",desc:"reading reflection, absorption and transmission from data and comparing a digital with an analog signal",html:()=>`<p class="big">A lamp sends 100 units of light at each material. One meter sits behind it and one beside the lamp.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Material</th><th>Passed through</th><th>Came back</th><th>Neither</th></tr>
     <tr><td>the display case's acrylic</td><td>93</td><td>5</td><td>2</td></tr>
     <tr><td>the hall's blackout curtain</td><td>0</td><td>8</td><td>92</td></tr>
     <tr><td>a sheet of white poster board</td><td>1</td><td>76</td><td>23</td></tr>
     <tr><td>a blue stage gel</td><td>29</td><td>11</td><td>60</td></tr>
     <tr><td>the hall's tinted skylight</td><td>61</td><td>15</td><td>24</td></tr></table>
     <p class="big">Two ways to get the exhibition's vote count from the hall to the office.<br>Method 1: a voltmeter on a line, the reading standing for the number of votes.<br>Method 2: the number sent as a pattern of ones and zeros, with the whole pattern repeated once.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("lamp",74)}${ico("palette",74)}</div>`,qs:["The blackout curtain and the white poster board both stop the light. Say how each one stops it and which of the two warms up more.","The blue gel passes 29 and absorbs 60. Say which colors it is absorbing and how the chart lets you know.","The line picks up a hum from the hall's lighting. Say which method still delivers the right vote count and why, then say what the repeat adds."],key:["The curtain absorbs: 92 of 100 was neither passed nor returned, so almost all the light is taken up in the fabric. The poster board reflects: 76 of 100 came straight back out, with only 23 absorbed. Both leave nothing on the far side, but by opposite routes. The curtain warms up far more, because absorbed light becomes heat in the material, and 92 units absorbed against 23 means roughly four times the heating. That is why a blackout curtain in sunlight gets hot and a white board stays cool. Partial: naming absorption and reflection correctly with no judgment given on which one warms more is approaching. (MS-PS4)","It is absorbing red, orange, yellow and green, which is most of the spectrum, and letting blue through. We know because it looks blue and the light that passes is what reaches the eye, and the chart says 60 of 100 was absorbed with only 29 passing. White light is a mixture, so a gel makes its color by removing the parts that are not that color, which is also why a deep gel is dim: three fifths of the light never gets through at all. Partial: naming the absorbed colors with nothing said about a gel making its color by removing the others is approaching. (MS-PS4)","The pattern of ones and zeros. It is a digital signal: each symbol only has to be told apart from the other one, so a hum that would shift a voltmeter needle leaves a one still clearly a one and the count arrives exactly as sent. The voltmeter is analog, where the value is the reading, so the hum changes the reading and the office writes down a wrong count with nothing to warn it. The repeat adds a check: if the two copies of the pattern do not match, the office knows at once that something was corrupted and can ask for it again, which is something a single voltmeter reading can never tell you. Partial: choosing the ones and zeros with nothing said about why the voltmeter hides its own error, or with no account of what the repeat adds, is approaching. (MS-PS4)"]}]},{id:31,title:"A Stand for the Model",short:"Design",parts:[{std:["MS-ETS1"],n:3,dur:85,dir:"Read the situation and the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"defining a design problem with criteria and constraints and evaluating competing designs against them",html:()=>`<p class="big">The exhibition needs a stand to hold a 9 kg model where 300 visitors can see it. The hall allows no fixings in the floor or the walls, there is $120 for materials and two evenings to build, and everything has to pass through a 1.2 m doorway. The exhibition has asked that the stand take a 120 N accidental push without tipping.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Design</th><th>Cost</th><th>Build time</th><th>Height of the model</th><th>Push it withstood</th><th>Width</th><th>Fixings needed</th></tr>
     <tr><td>A: cardboard tube column</td><td>$35</td><td>1 evening</td><td>1.3 m</td><td>40 N</td><td>0.4 m</td><td>none</td></tr>
     <tr><td>B: timber tripod</td><td>$110</td><td>2 evenings</td><td>1.5 m</td><td>160 N</td><td>0.9 m</td><td>none</td></tr>
     <tr><td>C: a wall mounted bracket</td><td>$60</td><td>1 evening</td><td>1.6 m</td><td>300 N</td><td>0.3 m</td><td>four screws in the wall</td></tr>
     <tr><td>D: a welded steel frame</td><td>$260</td><td>3 evenings</td><td>1.5 m</td><td>400 N</td><td>1.4 m</td><td>none</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("trophy",74)}${ico("ruler",74)}${ico("box",74)}</div>`,qs:["State this as a design problem, then sort the chart's columns, and name the figure that is the criterion the exhibition set.","Rule out the designs that cannot be used, naming for each one what rules it out.","Defend the design that is left using the chart, and name one thing you would test before the doors open."],key:["Achieve: hold a 9 kg model up where a crowd of 300 can see it, steadily enough that a knock does not bring it down. Criterion, the measure of success: the push the stand withstands without tipping, with 120 N as the level the exhibition set. Constraints, the limits on any solution: no more than $120 of materials, no more than two evenings, no fixings in the floor or walls, and nothing wider than the 1.2 m doorway. Height is useful but is not what the exhibition set a figure on. Partial: a goal stated with the 120 N filed as a constraint rather than as the threshold on the criterion is approaching. (MS-ETS1)","Design A fails the criterion: 40 N against the 120 N asked for, so a single bump would take it over. Design C fails a constraint: four screws in the wall, and fixings were ruled out, which is why its excellent 300 N does not save it. Design D fails three: $260 against $120, three evenings against two, and 1.4 m through a 1.2 m doorway. Only design B fails nothing. Partial: two of the three failing designs judged correctly is approaching, and D's three separate failures are usually counted as one. (MS-ETS1)","Design B: 160 N clears the 120 N the exhibition asked for, it needs no fixings, and at $110 and two evenings and 0.9 m wide it fits every limit, though with only $10 and no spare evening. Before the doors open, test the 160 N push on the hall's actual floor rather than a workshop bench, because a tripod's stability depends on its feet not sliding, and a polished hall floor can let the legs skate out from under a push that a rough floor would hold. Partial: defending Design B from the chart with nothing named that would be tested before the doors open is approaching. (MS-ETS1)"]},{std:["MS-ETS1"],n:3,dur:85,dir:"Look at the test results. Answer out loud.",desc:"analyzing test data to find which change helps and planning the next round of testing",html:()=>`<p class="big">Four versions of the tripod were pushed until they tipped.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Version</th><th>Leg spread</th><th>Model height</th><th>Ballast at the base</th><th>Push it withstood</th></tr>
     <tr><td>1</td><td>50 cm</td><td>1.5 m</td><td>none</td><td>70 N</td></tr>
     <tr><td>2</td><td>90 cm</td><td>1.5 m</td><td>none</td><td>160 N</td></tr>
     <tr><td>3</td><td>50 cm</td><td>1.5 m</td><td>6 kg</td><td>135 N</td></tr>
     <tr><td>4</td><td>50 cm</td><td>1.1 m</td><td>none</td><td>105 N</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("ruler",74)}${ico("trophy",74)}</div>`,qs:["Name the one change in each of versions 2, 3 and 4 against version 1, and the gain each one bought.","Rank the three changes by what they gained, and say which costs the exhibition least.","Specify a fifth version, predict the push it would withstand, and name the variable none of the four runs changed."],key:["Version 2 widened the leg spread from 50 cm to 90 cm and gained 90 N, from 70 N to 160 N. Version 3 added 6 kg of ballast at the base and gained 65 N, from 70 N to 135 N. Version 4 lowered the model from 1.5 m to 1.1 m and gained 35 N, from 70 N to 105 N. Each version moved one thing, which is why each gain can be credited to a cause. Partial: two of the three changes named with their gains is approaching. (MS-ETS1)","Leg spread gained the most at 90 N, then ballast at 65 N, then a lower model at 35 N. The leg spread also costs least: it needs no extra material beyond slightly longer feet, where ballast adds 6 kg that somebody has to carry and lift, and lowering the model costs 40 cm of visibility, which was the whole point of having a stand in a hall holding 300 people. So the cheapest change is also the best one, which does not always happen and is worth saying out loud. Partial: ranking the three gains correctly with nothing said about which one costs the exhibition least is approaching. (MS-ETS1)","Any version argued from the chart. The strongest keeps the model at 1.5 m and combines the two changes that cost the exhibition nothing it cares about: a 90 cm spread with 6 kg of ballast, predicting comfortably over 200 N, since the two gains were 90 N and 65 N separately and should add in large part. The variable none of the four runs changed is the floor surface: all four were pushed on the same floor, and a tripod that is wide and heavy still goes over if its feet slide, so the next test should hold version 2's settings and push it on bare boards, on carpet and on a polished floor. Partial: a fifth version argued from the chart with the floor surface not identified as the variable never changed is approaching. (MS-ETS1)"]}]}];const LEVELUPS={1:{id:"L1",title:"Level Up: Strong and Thorough",std:["RL.9-10.1"],needs:["RL.8.1"],n:1,dir:"Answer out loud. Read me the exact words for each detail.",html:()=>`<p class="big">Claim: the story treats being wrong in public as the thing worth having, not the clock. Prove it with strong and thorough evidence. Give at least three details from different parts of the story, and explain what each one shows.</p>${story()}`,key:["Three or more details from across the story, each explained. Examples: he had been told the clock was unreasonable and “had taken that as information rather than as advice,” so the project was never about getting it right first time. He argues with Ms. Vance, “It is level. I checked it,” and is wrong, in front of her. He writes the failure on a card in capitals and props it where everyone can read it. He adds “AND I KNOW WHY,” which turns the public failure into the thing he is offering. “People stopped. More than had stopped before.” And the closing judgment, that the afternoon was “the one part of the whole year he would have kept exactly as it happened,” which values the day including everything that went wrong in it. (HS RL.9-10.1, strong and thorough textual evidence)"]},2:{id:"L2",title:"Level Up: Take the Root",std:["A-REI.4"],needs:["8.EE.A.2"],n:3,dir:"Solve on your paper and hold it up. Give exact answers, then decimals where asked.",html:()=>`<ol class="qlist"><li>Solve (x − 3)<sup>2</sup> = 25.</li><li>Solve 2x<sup>2</sup> − 50 = 0.</li><li>Solve (x + 1)<sup>2</sup> = 12. Give the exact answers, then each one to the nearest hundredth.</li></ol>`,key:["x = 8 or x = −2. Taking the square root of both sides gives x − 3 = 5 or x − 3 = −5. Check: (8 − 3) squared = 25 and (−2 − 3) squared = 25. (HS A-REI.4b, solving by taking square roots)","x = 5 or x = −5. 2x squared = 50, so x squared = 25 and x = plus or minus 5. (HS A-REI.4b)","x = −1 plus or minus 2 times the square root of 3. The square root of 12 is 2 times the square root of 3, which is about 3.46, so x is about 2.46 or about −4.46. (HS A-REI.4b, exact and approximate solutions)"]},4:{id:"L4",title:"Level Up: Angles on the Ramp",std:["G-SRT.8"],needs:["8.G.B.7"],n:2,dir:"Work on your paper with a calculator. Show the ratio you used and hold it up.",mats:["a calculator"],html:()=>`<ol class="qlist"><li>The ramp rises 1.5 m over a horizontal run of 3.6 m. What angle does it make with the ground, to the nearest tenth of a degree?</li><li>A banner 17 ft long is hung corner to corner on a wall 15 ft long. What angle does the banner make with the bottom edge of the wall, to the nearest degree? Then use the Pythagorean theorem to check the height of the wall.</li></ol>`,key:["About 22.6 degrees. The rise is opposite the angle and the run is next to it, so tan of the angle = 1.5 ÷ 3.6 = 0.41667, and the angle is 22.62 degrees, which rounds to 22.6. (HS G-SRT.8, a trigonometric ratio in an applied problem)","About 28 degrees, and the wall is 8 ft tall. The 15 ft edge is next to the angle and the 17 ft banner is the hypotenuse, so cos of the angle = 15 ÷ 17 = 0.88235, and the angle is 28.07 degrees, which rounds to 28. Check with the theorem: 17 squared − 15 squared = 289 − 225 = 64, and the square root of 64 is 8. (HS G-SRT.8, a trigonometric ratio and the Pythagorean theorem together)"]},5:{id:"L5",title:"Level Up: Two Wants at Once",std:["RL.9-10.3"],needs:["RL.8.3"],n:2,dir:"Answer out loud. Use details from the story.",html:()=>`<ol class="qlist"><li>Toby wants two things that pull against each other. Name both, and give a detail from the story for each one.</li><li>How does Ms. Vance's advice change him over the course of the afternoon? What idea does that development bring out?</li></ol>${story()}`,key:["He wants the clock to be right, which is why he checks the escapement twice, checks the rod for a bend, levels the table with folded cards, and says “So I should have put it on the stage.” He also wants the clock to be seen, which is why he brought it to a hall rather than a quiet room and why Ms. Vance's reply, “Then twelve people would have seen it,” lands hard enough to stop him talking. The crowd that proves one want is the thing destroying the other. Needs both wants and a detail for each. (HS RL.9-10.3, a complex character with conflicting motivations)","She refuses to let him choose between the two wants and tells him to publish the conflict instead: “Leave it. Write the number on a card.” He moves from hiding the error, to labeling it, to explaining it forty times, to standing with strangers and watching the second hand. The idea that brings out is that a piece of work can be worth more as a demonstration of what is true than as a thing that performed correctly, and that the people who stop are stopping for the explanation and not for the clock. (HS RL.9-10.3, how a character develops and advances the theme)"]},8:{id:"L8",title:"Level Up: Eliminate Both Ways",std:["A-REI.6"],needs:["8.EE.C.8"],n:2,dir:"Solve on your paper and hold it up. Show your steps and check each answer in both equations.",html:()=>`<ol class="qlist"><li>Solve this pair: 3x + 4y = 10 and 5x − 2y = 21.</li><li>Solve this pair: 6x + 5y = 7 and 4x + 3y = 5.</li></ol>`,key:["x = 4 and y = −1/2, or (4, −0.5). Steps: multiply the second equation by 2 to get 10x − 4y = 42, then add it to the first: 13x = 52, so x = 4. Then 3(4) + 4y = 10 gives 4y = −2 and y = −1/2. Check in the second: 5(4) − 2(−0.5) = 20 + 1 = 21. (HS A-REI.6)","x = 2 and y = −1, or (2, −1). Steps: multiply the first equation by 3 to get 18x + 15y = 21, and the second by 5 to get 20x + 15y = 25. Subtract: 2x = 4, so x = 2. Then 4(2) + 3y = 5 gives 3y = −3 and y = −1. Check in the first: 6(2) + 5(−1) = 12 − 5 = 7. Both equations had to be multiplied. (HS A-REI.6)"]},10:{id:"L10",title:"Level Up: Fit a Line",std:["S-ID.6"],needs:["8.SP.A.1"],n:3,dir:"Work on your paper and hold it up. Leave the outlier out of the line, and say that you did.",html:()=>`<p class="big">Use the ten points that follow the pattern. Leave the point at 66 feet out.</p>${bigTable(["Distance, feet","5","12","20","28","35","44","52","60","72","85"],[["People who stopped","96","90","83","76","70","62","55","48","37","25"]],"The ten tables that follow the pattern")}<ol class="qlist"><li>Fit a straight line to these ten points. Give its slope and its vertical intercept, and say what each one means here.</li><li>Use your line to predict how many people would stop at a table 95 feet from the door. Is that prediction trustworthy? Why or why not?</li><li>Judge the fit. Name two things you looked at, and say what the point at (66, 81) does to your judgment of the fit.</li></ol>`,key:["Any reasonable fitted line, with both numbers interpreted. Using the first and last points, the slope is (25 − 96) ÷ (85 − 5) = −71 ÷ 80, which is about −0.89 people per foot: roughly nine fewer people stopping for every ten feet further from the door. The vertical intercept is about 96 + 0.89 × 5, which is about 100: the number the line predicts for a table right at the door. Accept any slope between about −0.85 and −0.95 with a matching intercept near 100. (HS S-ID.6a, fitting a linear function and S-ID.7, interpreting slope and intercept in context)","About 15 or 16 people, from roughly 100 − 0.89 × 95. It is not trustworthy, because 95 feet is outside the range of the data, which stops at 85 feet. The line is only evidence about the stretch it was fitted to, and there is no reason to assume the pattern continues, especially since the count cannot fall below zero. (HS S-ID.6a, using a fitted function, and the limits of extrapolation)","Any two, such as: whether the points sit close to the line all the way along rather than bending away at one end; whether the residuals, the gaps between each point and the line, are small and show no pattern; and whether any single point is doing most of the work. For these ten the fit is very good, with every point within about a person of the line. (66, 81) does not weaken that judgment, because it was left out on purpose and for a stated reason, but it has to be reported alongside the line, since a reader who saw only the line would not know that one table broke the pattern by a large margin. (HS S-ID.6b, analyzing residuals and assessing the fit)"]},11:{id:"L11",title:"Level Up: Phrase It Together",std:["L.9-10.1"],needs:["L.8.1"],n:3,dir:"Say each new sentence out loud, then name the phrase or clause you used.",html:()=>`<ol class="qlist"><li>Combine these into one sentence using a participial phrase: “The card stood against the base. It said that the clock was nineteen minutes slow.”</li><li>Combine these into one sentence using a subordinate clause beginning with Because: “The floor moved a sixteenth of an inch. The pendulum lost a little on every swing.”</li><li>Combine these into one sentence using an appositive phrase: “Ms. Vance named the cause in one word. She has run the building for twenty-two years.”</li></ol>`,key:["“Standing against the base, the card said that the clock was nineteen minutes slow.” Names the participial phrase: Standing against the base. Accept any correct version built on a participial phrase. (HS L.9-10.1, various types of phrases and clauses)","“Because the floor moved a sixteenth of an inch, the pendulum lost a little on every swing.” Names it as a subordinate clause. (HS L.9-10.1)","“Ms. Vance, who has run the building for twenty-two years, named the cause in one word” uses a relative clause; the appositive version is “Ms. Vance, the caretaker of twenty-two years, named the cause in one word.” Full credit needs an appositive phrase, a noun phrase renaming Ms. Vance, and the student naming it. (HS L.9-10.1, various types of phrases and clauses)"]},14:{id:"L14",title:"Level Up: Hold the Line",std:["W.9-10.1"],needs:["W.8.1"],n:2,dir:"Add to the writing on your paper, or say it out loud.",html:()=>`<p class="big">Add two or three sentences to the argument you just wrote.</p><ol class="qlist"><li>State the strongest version of the opposing claim, using a real number from the fact box, and then point out what that side leaves out.</li><li>Rewrite your closing so that it follows from your reasoning and keeps a formal, objective tone with no second person.</li></ol>`,key:["States the opposing claim at its strongest, with a real figure from the fact box rather than a weakened version, and then names what that side leaves out, such as the exam booking until 4 June on one side or the 3,100 usable square feet on the other. (HS W.9-10.1a and W.9-10.1b, developing claims and counterclaims fairly)","The new closing follows from the argument the student actually made, supports it rather than repeating the claim, and keeps a formal, objective tone with no slang and no address to the reader. (HS W.9-10.1d and W.9-10.1e, formal objective tone and conclusion)"]}};const READ_STD=["RL.8.1","RL.8.3","RL.8.4","RI.8.1","RI.8.9","RL.9-10.1","RL.9-10.3","RI.8.8"];const WRITE_STD=["W.8.1","W.8.2","W.9-10.1","W.8.3"];const MATH_STD=["8.NS.A.1","8.EE.A.2","8.EE.C.8","8.G.B.7","8.SP.A.1","A-REI.4","A-REI.6","G-SRT.8","S-ID.6"];const EXT_MATH=["8.EE.A.1","8.EE.B.5","8.F.A.1","8.F.A.3","8.G.A.3"];const TEXT_STD=READ_STD.concat(["W.8.8"]);const LOG_NAMES={readq:"Questions read aloud",passage:"Text read aloud by an adult",repeat:"Directions repeated or reworded",vocab:"Word or phrase explained by teacher",hint:"Hint or first step given",fewer:"Fewer questions",time:"Extra time",scribe:"Adult wrote the student's words",brk:"Break"};function stopLogs(st){const out=["readq"];if(READ_STD.concat(["W.8.8","SL.8.4"]).some(x=>st.std.includes(x)))out.push("passage");out.push("repeat","vocab","hint","fewer","time");if(WRITE_STD.concat(["W.8.8"]).some(x=>st.std.includes(x)))out.push("scribe");out.push("brk");return out}const dl=a=>`<dl>${a.map(x=>`<dt>${x[0]}</dt><dd>${x[1]}</dd>`).join("")}</dl>`;const ul=a=>`<ul class="card-list">${a.map(x=>`<li>${x}</li>`).join("")}</ul>`;function wt(st){return st.std.includes("W.8.8")?"notes":st.std.includes("SL.8.4")?"say":st.std.includes("W.8.3")?"story":st.std.includes("W.8.2")?"explain":"argument"}function twoCol(st){return st.id===9}function stopSpeak(){try{window.speechSynthesis&&window.speechSynthesis.cancel()}catch(e){}}function guardSpeech(){["mapBtn","teacherBtn","newBtn","prevBtn","gemBtn","skipBtn"].forEach(i=>{const el=document.getElementById(i);if(el&&!el.dataset.tts){el.dataset.tts="1";el.addEventListener("click",stopSpeak)}})}function speakText(t){stopSpeak();guardSpeech();try{const u=new SpeechSynthesisUtterance(t);u.rate=+(document.getElementById("ttsRate")||{value:.9}).value;window.speechSynthesis.speak(u)}catch(e){}}function textOf(sel){return[...document.querySelectorAll(sel)].map(e=>e.textContent.trim()).join(" ")}function listenHtml(st){const two=twoCol(st);return`<div class="blk-row">${two?`<button class="chip" data-say="a">Play Text X</button><button class="chip" data-say="b">Play Text Y</button>`:`<button class="chip" data-say="p">Play the text</button>`}<button class="chip" data-say="q">Play the questions</button><button class="chip" data-say="x">Stop</button></div><label class="small">Speed <select id="ttsRate"><option value="0.75">Slow</option><option value="0.9" selected>Normal</option><option value="1.1">Fast</option></select></label><p class="small">Uses this computer's voice. If you hear nothing, check the volume, then read the text aloud yourself and mark “Text read aloud by an adult.”</p>`}function listenBind(b){b.querySelectorAll("[data-say]").forEach(x=>x.onclick=()=>{const k=x.dataset.say,card="#stopView .card ";if(k==="x")return stopSpeak();if(k==="p")speakText(textOf(card+".passage"));if(k==="a")speakText(textOf(card+".texts article:nth-child(1)"));if(k==="b")speakText(textOf(card+".texts article:nth-child(2)"));if(k==="q")speakText(textOf(card+"ol.qlist > li:not([hidden]), "+card+"> .big"))})}const G_STORY=[["pendulum","a weighted rod that swings freely and keeps a steady beat"],["escapement","the part of a clock that lets the gears move on by one step with each swing"],["bob","the weight at the bottom of a pendulum"],["dolly","a low platform on wheels for moving heavy things"],["level","flat, with no side higher than the other"],["rigid","stiff, not giving at all under a push"],["index card","a stiff blank card about three inches by five"]];const G_CLOCK=[["period","the time one full swing takes"],["expand","to get larger, here because of heat"],["friction","the drag that slows a moving part and turns its energy into heat"],["masonry","brick or stone built into a wall"],["suspended floor","a floor carried on beams with space underneath, so it can flex"],["vibration","small fast shaking passed through a solid"],["metrology","the science of measurement"]];const G_REPORT=[["clicker","a hand counter you press once for each person"],["fire lane","floor that has to stay clear so people can get out"],["clearance","space that has to be kept empty around a door or a route"],["usable floor area","the floor left once the clear routes are taken out"],["re-entry","a person coming back in after stepping out"],["bottleneck","a narrow place where a flow backs up"],["interpretation","what somebody says the facts mean, as opposed to the facts"]];const G_DATA=[["scatter plot","a graph with one point for each case, placed at its two values"],["association","a pattern in which one quantity tends to change with another"],["linear association","points that cluster along a straight line"],["outlier","a point that sits far away from the pattern"],["residual","the gap between a point and the line fitted to the data"],["extrapolate","to use a line outside the range of the data it was fitted to"],["rational number","a number that can be written as one whole number over another"],["irrational number","a number that cannot, and whose decimal never ends or repeats"]];const G_WRITE={argument:[["claim","what you want the reader to believe or do"],["counterclaim","the strongest thing the other side would say"],["relevant evidence","facts that actually bear on the claim"],["formal style","writing with no slang, no texting language, and no jokes to the reader"],["objective tone","sticking to the case, with no personal digs and no exclamations"],["disputed figure","a number the two sources do not agree on, which has to be reported as disputed"]],explain:[["domain-specific vocabulary","the technical words a subject uses"],["transition","a word or phrase that shows how two ideas connect"],["concrete detail","a specific fact, number or example rather than a general statement"]],story:[["point of view","who is telling the story"],["pacing","how fast or slow the story moves through its events"],["sensory detail","a detail of what can be seen, heard, felt, smelled or tasted"],["reflection","a sentence where the narrator says what the events meant"]],notes:[["search terms","the words you type to find sources"],["source","where a fact came from"],["quotation marks","the marks that show you are using someone's exact words"],["plagiarism","using someone else's words or ideas as if they were your own"],["paraphrase","to say it in your own words"]],say:[["recommendation","what you are telling the board to do"],["drawback","the cost or weakness of your own choice"],["disputed","not agreed on by the two sources, so it cannot be quoted as settled"]]};function glossHtml(st){if(st.id===14)return dl(G_WRITE[wt(st)]||G_WRITE.argument);if(st.id===12)return dl(G_WRITE.notes.concat(G_CLOCK));if(st.id===13)return dl(G_WRITE.say.concat(G_REPORT));if(st.id===3)return dl(G_CLOCK);if(st.id===9)return dl(G_REPORT);if(st.std.includes("RI.8.8"))return dl(G_REPORT.concat(G_CLOCK));if(MATH_STD.some(x=>st.std.includes(x))||EXT_MATH.some(x=>st.std.includes(x)))return dl(G_DATA);return dl(G_STORY)}const TERMS=[["cite","point to the exact words in the text that prove your answer"],["strongest evidence","the words that prove the answer best, not just words on the same topic"],["explicit","stated right in the text"],["inference","something the text does not say outright, but that you can figure out from clues"],["connotation","the feeling a word carries beyond its plain meaning"],["dialogue","the words characters say out loud"],["incident","one event in a story"],["propel the action","push the story forward so that something new has to happen"],["motivation","what a character wants, and why"],["fact or interpretation","a fact can be checked; an interpretation is what someone thinks the facts mean"],["thorough evidence","enough details, from more than one place in the text"]];const FRAMES={cite:["The answer is ____. The words that prove it are “____.”","The strongest evidence is “____” because ____.","I can infer that ____ because the text says “____.”"],"RL.8.3":["When ____ says “____,” it makes ____ decide to ____.","Because ____ happened, ____ now has to ____.","This line shows that ____ is ____ because ____."],"RL.8.4":["The word ____ suggests ____, while ____ would suggest ____.","The author chose ____ instead of ____, which has the effect of ____.","This phrase means ____, and I know because ____."],"RI.8.9":["Text X says ____, but Text Y says ____. That is a disagreement about a fact.","They both agree that ____, but Text X reads it as ____ while Text Y reads it as ____.","To settle the fact you would need ____.","No evidence settles the other one because ____."],math:["The two lines cross at ____, which means ____.","The association is ____ and ____.","The outlier is ____, which sits ____ away from the pattern.","This number is ____ because ____."],"RI.8.8":["The claim is ____.","The reasons offered are ____.","The evidence is ____, which comes from ____.","This piece does not bear on the claim because ____."],math2:["The unit rate is ____, which on the graph is ____.","It is a function because ____.","The equation is y = ____x + ____, where the ____ means ____.","After the ____, the points are ____."]};function framesHtml(st){const k=st.std.includes("RI.8.9")?"RI.8.9":st.std.includes("RL.8.4")?"RL.8.4":st.std.includes("RL.8.3")||st.std.includes("RL.9-10.3")?"RL.8.3":st.std.includes("RI.8.8")?"RI.8.8":EXT_MATH.some(x=>st.std.includes(x))?"math2":MATH_STD.some(x=>st.std.includes(x))?"math":"cite";return`<ul class="starters" style="font-size:1.2rem">${FRAMES[k].map(x=>`<li>${x}</li>`).join("")}</ul>`}function gramHtml(){return`<h4>Verbals: verb forms doing another job</h4>`+dl([["Gerund","ends in -ing and acts as a noun. <em>Leveling the clock took two cards.</em>"],["Participle","often ends in -ing or -ed and describes a noun. <em>The failing escapement clicked.</em>"],["Infinitive","to plus a verb. <em>His plan was to start it at eight.</em>"]])+`<h4>Voice</h4>`+dl([["Active","the subject does the action. <em>Toby propped the card.</em>"],["Passive","the subject receives the action. <em>The card was propped by Toby.</em>"]])+`<h4>Mood</h4>`+dl([["Indicative","states a fact. <em>The floor is soft.</em>"],["Imperative","gives a command. <em>Write the number on a card.</em>"],["Interrogative","asks a question. <em>Is the floor soft?</em>"],["Conditional","tells what would or could happen. <em>I would move it if there were room.</em>"],["Subjunctive","a wish or something not true. Uses were. <em>If the floor were solid, it would keep time.</em>"]])+`<h4>Phrases and clauses</h4>`+dl([["Participial phrase","a participle plus its words, describing a noun. <em>Standing against the base, the card said so.</em>"],["Subordinate clause","has a subject and a verb but cannot stand alone. <em>Because the floor moved, the swing arrived late.</em>"],["Appositive phrase","renames the noun right beside it. <em>Ms. Vance, the caretaker of twenty-two years, said one word.</em>"]])+`<p class="small">Keep the same voice and the same mood all the way through a sentence.</p>`}const PLAN={argument:["My claim","Reason 1 and the evidence from the fact box","Reason 2 and the evidence from the fact box","The other side's claim, and my answer to it","Conclusion"],explain:["My topic","Cause group 1: the rod","Cause group 2: lost energy","Terms I need to define","Conclusion"],story:["Who is telling it, and where","What they expected","What actually happened","What they decided, and what it meant"],notes:["Which source I trust, and why","My search terms","My quote or paraphrase","My citation"],say:["My recommendation","My strongest evidence from the table","The drawback","What I need from the board"]};function planHtml(st){const S=E.state;S.plan=S.plan||{};const k=wt(st),v=S.plan[k]||[];return`<div class="planner">${PLAN[k].map((x,i)=>`<label>${x}<textarea data-pi="${i}">${(v[i]||"").replace(/</g,"&lt;")}</textarea></label>`).join("")}</div><p class="small">Your plan is saved for the speaking stop and the writing stop.</p>`}function planBind(b,r,st){b.querySelectorAll("[data-pi]").forEach(t=>t.oninput=()=>{const S=E.state,k=wt(st);S.plan=S.plan||{};S.plan[k]=S.plan[k]||[];S.plan[k][+t.dataset.pi]=t.value;E.save()})}const SENT={argument:{s:["The board should choose ____ because ____.","The fact box shows that ____.","Some would argue that ____. However, ____.","The visitor figures are disputed: ____ says ____ and ____ says ____.","For these reasons, ____."],w:["because","for example","however","although","in addition","as a result","therefore","on the other hand"]},explain:{s:["A pendulum clock loses time when ____.","The first group of causes is ____.","As a result, ____.","____, which means ____.","In short, ____."],w:["first","second","because","as a result","by contrast","in short"]},story:{s:["I had expected ____.","At first, ____.","Then ____.","“____,” ____ said.","Looking back, ____."],w:["at first","then","after that","a moment later","by the time","in the end"]},notes:{s:["I trust Source ____ more because ____.","These search terms are best because ____.","Dr. Soraya Lindqvist reports, “____.”","According to Dr. Soraya Lindqvist, ____.","Author. “Title.” Publisher, year."],w:["according to","reports","states","explains"]},say:{s:["The board should use ____.","My strongest evidence is ____.","That figure is disputed, so ____.","The drawback is ____.","From the board I need ____."],w:["because","the table shows","compared with","by contrast","for that reason"]}};function sentHtml(st){const d=SENT[wt(st)]||SENT.argument;return`<ul class="starters">${d.s.map(x=>`<li>${x}</li>`).join("")}</ul><h4>Linking words</h4><div class="wordbank">${d.w.map(x=>`<span>${x}</span>`).join("")}</div>`}const CHECK={story:["I set the scene and said who is telling it.","The events are in an order that makes sense.","I used dialogue or description.","I used time words.","My ending reflects on what happened.","I reread it for mistakes."],argument:["I stated my claim.","I named the other side and showed how my claim is different.","I gave reasons with evidence from the fact box.","I treated the disputed numbers as disputed.","I used linking words.","I kept a formal, objective style.","I ended with a conclusion.","I reread it for mistakes."],explain:["I named my topic and previewed it.","I kept the two groups of causes separate.","I used facts and details from the article.","I defined every technical term I used.","I used transitions.","I kept a formal style.","I ended with a conclusion.","I reread it for mistakes."]};function checkHtml(st){return`<ul class="card-list" style="list-style:none;padding-left:0">${(CHECK[wt(st)]||CHECK.argument).map(x=>`<li><label style="display:flex;gap:8px;align-items:flex-start"><input type="checkbox" style="width:20px;height:20px;margin-top:4px"> ${x}</label></li>`).join("")}</ul>`}let calcE="",calcAns="";function calcEval(){let e=calcE.replace(/×/g,"*").replace(/÷/g,"/").replace(/−/g,"-").replace(/\^/g,"**").replace(/√\(/g,"Math.sqrt(");if(!/^[0-9+\-*/().\sMathsqr]*$/.test(e)||!e)return"";try{const o=(e.match(/\(/g)||[]).length-(e.match(/\)/g)||[]).length;if(o>0)e+=")".repeat(o);const v=Function('"use strict";return ('+e+")")();return Number.isFinite(v)?String(+v.toPrecision(10)):"Error"}catch(x){return"Error"}}function calcHtml(){const keys=["7","8","9","÷","⌫","4","5","6","×","(","1","2","3","−",")","0",".","^","+","C","√(","x²","="];return`<div class="calc-disp" aria-live="polite">${calcE||"0"}${calcAns!==""?`<br><strong>= ${calcAns}</strong>`:""}</div><div class="calc-keys">${keys.map(k=>`<button data-k="${k}" class="${k==="="?"eq":/[0-9.]/.test(k)?"":"op"}">${k==="^"?"xʸ":k==="√("?"√":k}</button>`).join("")}</div><p class="small">Press √ then the number and close the bracket. For a square, press x². This calculator has no sine, cosine, or tangent key.</p>`}function calcBind(b,r){b.querySelectorAll("[data-k]").forEach(x=>x.onclick=()=>{const k=x.dataset.k;if(k==="C"){calcE="";calcAns=""}else if(k==="⌫"){calcE=calcE.replace(/(√\(|.)$/,"");calcAns=""}else if(k==="="){calcAns=calcEval()}else{if(calcAns!==""&&calcAns!=="Error"&&/^[+−×÷^x]/.test(k))calcE=calcAns;else if(calcAns!=="")calcE="";calcAns="";calcE+=k==="x²"?"^2":k}r()})}const MULT_N=12;let multSel=[0,0];function multHtml(){let h=`<div class="tool-scroll"><table class="mult"><tr><th>×</th>`;for(let c=1;c<=MULT_N;c++)h+=`<th data-r="0" data-c="${c}" class="${multSel[1]===c?"hl":""}">${c}</th>`;h+="</tr>";for(let r=1;r<=MULT_N;r++){h+=`<tr><th data-r="${r}" data-c="0" class="${multSel[0]===r?"hl":""}">${r}</th>`;for(let c=1;c<=MULT_N;c++){const hr=multSel[0]===r,hc=multSel[1]===c;h+=`<td data-r="${r}" data-c="${c}" class="${hr&&hc?"pick":hr||hc?"hl":""}">${r*c}</td>`}h+="</tr>"}return h+`</table></div><p class="small">Click a number to light up its row and column. Click it again to clear.</p>`}function sqHtml(){let h=`<div class="tool-scroll"><table class="mult"><tr><th>n</th>`;for(let n=1;n<=20;n++)h+=`<th>${n}</th>`;h+=`</tr><tr><th>n²</th>`;for(let n=1;n<=20;n++)h+=`<td>${n*n}</td>`;h+=`</tr><tr><th>n³</th>`;for(let n=1;n<=10;n++)h+=`<td>${n*n*n}</td>`;for(let n=11;n<=20;n++)h+=`<td></td>`;return h+`</tr></table></div><p class="small">To find a square root, find the number in the n² row and look up to n. 289 is in the row, so the square root of 289 is 17. The n³ row is filled to 10.</p>`}const rightTri=`<svg viewBox="0 0 220 130" width="220" role="img" aria-label="Right triangle with legs a and b and hypotenuse c" style="display:block;margin:0 auto"><polygon points="20,110 190,110 20,15" fill="var(--sea-soft)" stroke="var(--fig-ink)" stroke-width="3"/><rect x="20" y="96" width="14" height="14" fill="none" stroke="var(--fig-ink)" stroke-width="2"/><text x="105" y="128" text-anchor="middle" font-size="18" font-weight="700" fill="var(--ink)">b</text><text x="8" y="68" text-anchor="middle" font-size="18" font-weight="700" fill="var(--ink)">a</text><text x="116" y="54" font-size="18" font-weight="700" fill="var(--ink)">c</text></svg>`;const rules=rows=>`<table class="meas">${rows.map(r=>`<tr><th>${r[0]}</th></tr><tr><td class="formula">${r[1]}</td></tr>`).join("")}</table>`;const FORM={"8.EE.C.8":["Pairs of linear equations",ul(["<strong>Graphing:</strong> the solution is the point where the two lines cross. Read off (x, y).","<strong>Substitution:</strong> when both are solved for y, set the two right sides equal, solve for x, then find y.","<strong>Elimination:</strong> add or subtract the equations so one variable cancels. Multiply an equation first if you need to.","<strong>One solution:</strong> different slopes, so the lines cross once.","<strong>No solution:</strong> same slope, different y-intercepts, so the lines are parallel.","<strong>Infinitely many:</strong> one equation is a multiple of the other, so it is the same line.","Always check your answer in <em>both</em> equations."])],"8.EE.A.2":["Squares and cubes",`<p class="formula">x² = p &nbsp;&nbsp; x = ±√p</p><p class="formula">x³ = p &nbsp;&nbsp; x = ∛p</p>`+ul(["A square equation has two solutions, one positive and one negative, because squaring destroys the sign.","A cube equation has one real solution, because cubing keeps the sign.","A length, an area side, or an edge cannot be negative, so only the positive root answers those questions.","√2 is irrational: its decimal never ends and never repeats."])],"8.NS.A.1":["Rational and irrational",ul(["A <strong>rational</strong> number can be written as one whole number over another. Its decimal ends or repeats.","An <strong>irrational</strong> number cannot. Its decimal never ends and never repeats. Examples: π, √2, √10.","<strong>Repeating decimal to a fraction:</strong> call it x, multiply by 10 for a one-digit block or 100 for a two-digit block, subtract, and solve.","The square root of a whole number is rational only when that number is a perfect square."])],"8.G.B.7":["Pythagorean Theorem",rightTri+`<p class="formula">a² + b² = c²</p>`+ul(["a and b are the legs. c is the hypotenuse, the longest side, across from the right angle.","To find a leg instead: c² − a² = b².","On a grid, the legs are the horizontal and vertical distances between the two points.","For a box: length² + width² + height² = diagonal²."])],"8.SP.A.1":["Scatter plots",ul(["One point is one case, placed at its two values.","Look for <strong>clustering</strong>, <strong>outliers</strong>, a direction up or down, and whether the points follow a line.","<strong>Positive</strong> association: both go up together. <strong>Negative</strong>: one goes up as the other goes down.","An outlier with a known cause is evidence, not an error. Report it; do not delete it.","Association is not cause."])],"A-REI.4":["Solving by taking square roots",ul(["Get the squared expression alone, then take the square root of both sides and keep both signs.","(x + k)² = p gives x + k = ±√p, so x = −k ± √p.","Simplify the radical where you can: √12 = 2√3."])],"G-SRT.8":["Right triangle trigonometry",rules([["Sine","sin θ = opposite ÷ hypotenuse"],["Cosine","cos θ = adjacent ÷ hypotenuse"],["Tangent","tan θ = opposite ÷ adjacent"]])+ul(["Label the sides from the angle you are working with first.","To find an angle from a ratio, use the inverse key on a calculator.","The Pythagorean theorem still holds and is the quickest check."])],"S-ID.6":["Fitting a line to data",ul(["Draw a line that runs through the middle of the points with about as many above as below.","<strong>Slope</strong> = change in y ÷ change in x, and it carries units: so many people per foot.","The <strong>vertical intercept</strong> is what the line predicts when x is 0. Say whether that is meaningful here.","A <strong>residual</strong> is the gap between a point and the line. Small residuals with no pattern mean a good fit.","Predicting outside the range of the data is extrapolation, and it is not supported by the data."])]};FORM["A-REI.6"]=FORM["8.EE.C.8"];FORM["8.EE.A.1"]=["Integer exponents",ul(["Multiplying: add the exponents. Dividing: subtract them.","A negative exponent means one over the positive power: 3^-3 = 1/27.","Anything non-zero to the power 0 is 1.","A power of a power multiplies the exponents."])];FORM["8.EE.B.5"]=["Proportional graphs",ul(["A proportional relationship graphs as a straight line through the origin.","The <strong>unit rate</strong> is the slope, and it is the y value at x = 1.","To compare two relationships shown in different ways, turn both into a rate per one unit first."])];FORM["8.F.A.1"]=["Functions",ul(["A function assigns <strong>exactly one</strong> output to each input.","Two different outputs for the same input means it is not a function.","The graph of a function is the set of ordered pairs (input, output)."])];FORM["8.F.A.3"]=["Linear and non-linear",`<p class="formula">y = mx + b</p>`+ul(["m is the rate of change, the slope. b is the value when x is 0, the vertical intercept.","Linear means equal steps in x give equal steps in y, so the graph is a straight line.","A = s^2 is not linear: (1, 1), (2, 4) and (3, 9) do not lie on one line."])];FORM["8.G.A.3"]=["Transformations by coordinates",ul(["<strong>Translation:</strong> add the same amount to every x and every y.","<strong>Reflection across the x-axis:</strong> (x, y) becomes (x, -y). <strong>Across the y-axis:</strong> (x, y) becomes (-x, y).","<strong>Rotation 90 degrees counterclockwise about the origin:</strong> (x, y) becomes (-y, x). <strong>Rotation 180 degrees about the origin:</strong> (x, y) becomes (-x, -y).","<strong>Dilation from the origin by k:</strong> multiply both coordinates by k. Lengths scale by k and angles do not change.","Translations, reflections and rotations keep lengths and angles. Only a dilation changes size."])];function formHtml(st){const seen=[];return st.std.filter(x=>FORM[x]).map(x=>{if(seen.includes(FORM[x][0]))return"";seen.push(FORM[x][0]);return`<h4>${FORM[x][0]}</h4>${FORM[x][1]}`}).join("")}const EX={"8.EE.C.8":[["Solve a pair by substitution",["Pair: y = 5x − 2 and y = x + 10.","Both are solved for y, so set the right sides equal: 5x − 2 = x + 10.","Solve: 4x = 12, so x = 3.","Put x = 3 into either equation: y = 3 + 10 = 13.","Solution (3, 13). Check in the other: 5(3) − 2 = 13."]],["Solve a pair by elimination",["Pair: 3x + y = 14 and x − y = 2.","Add the two equations so y cancels: 4x = 16, so x = 4.","Put x = 4 back in: 4 − y = 2, so y = 2.","Solution (4, 2). Check: 3(4) + 2 = 14."]]],"8.NS.A.1":[["Turn a repeating decimal into a fraction",["Take 0.1818 repeating. Call it x.","The repeating block is two digits, so multiply by 100: 100x = 18.1818 repeating.","Subtract: 100x − x = 18, so 99x = 18.","x = 18/99 = 2/11."]]],"8.G.B.7":[["Find a missing side",["Legs are 7 and 24. Find the hypotenuse c.","7² + 24² = c²","49 + 576 = 625","c is the square root of 625, which is 25.","To find a leg instead, subtract: c² − a² = b²."]]]};EX["A-REI.6"]=EX["8.EE.C.8"];function exHtml(st){const seen=[];return st.std.filter(x=>EX[x]).map(x=>EX[x].map(e=>{if(seen.includes(e[0]))return"";seen.push(e[0]);return`<h4>${e[0]}</h4><ol class="card-list">${e[1].map(s=>`<li>${s}</li>`).join("")}</ol>`}).join("")).join("")+`<p class="small">These are different problems from the ones on the stop. Use the same steps.</p>`}let gridPts=[],gridJoin=false;const GS=440,GM=10,GU=(GS-68)/(2*GM);function pgridHtml(){const lines=[];if(gridJoin)for(let i=1;i<gridPts.length;i++)lines.push([gridPts[i-1],gridPts[i]]);return`<div class="blk-row"><button class="chip" data-g="join" aria-pressed="${gridJoin}">Connect the points</button><button class="chip" data-g="undo">Undo</button><button class="chip" data-g="clear">Clear</button></div><div class="figure" data-pg style="cursor:crosshair">${F.coordGrid({max:GM,quad4:true,size:GS,points:gridPts,lines})}</div><p class="small">Click the grid to plot a point. Points: ${gridPts.map(p=>`(${p[0]}, ${p[1]})`).join(", ")||"none yet"}</p>`}function pgridBind(b,r){b.querySelectorAll("[data-g]").forEach(x=>x.onclick=()=>{const g=x.dataset.g;if(g==="join")gridJoin=!gridJoin;if(g==="undo")gridPts.pop();if(g==="clear")gridPts=[];r()});const el=b.querySelector("[data-pg] svg");if(!el)return;el.onclick=e=>{const q=el.getBoundingClientRect(),vx=(e.clientX-q.left)*GS/q.width,vy=(e.clientY-q.top)*GS/q.height,x=Math.round((vx-34)/GU)-GM,y=Math.round((GS-34-vy)/GU)-GM;if(Math.abs(x)>GM||Math.abs(y)>GM)return;gridPts.push([x,y]);r()}}const TOOLS={listen:{name:"Computer reads it aloud",std:TEXT_STD,ok:()=>"speechSynthesis"in window,html:listenHtml,bind:listenBind},gloss:{name:"Word help",std:TEXT_STD.concat(WRITE_STD,MATH_STD,["SL.8.4"]),html:glossHtml},terms:{name:"Reading terms",std:READ_STD,html:()=>dl(TERMS)},frames:{name:"Answer frames",std:READ_STD.concat(MATH_STD,EXT_MATH),html:framesHtml},gram:{name:"Grammar card",std:["L.8.1","L.9-10.1"],html:gramHtml},plan:{name:"Planner",std:WRITE_STD.concat(["W.8.8","SL.8.4"]),html:planHtml,bind:planBind},sent:{name:"Sentence frames",std:WRITE_STD.concat(["W.8.8","SL.8.4"]),html:sentHtml},check:{name:"Writing checklist",std:WRITE_STD,html:checkHtml},calc:{name:"Calculator",std:MATH_STD.concat(EXT_MATH),html:calcHtml,bind:calcBind},mult:{name:"Multiplication chart",std:MATH_STD.concat(EXT_MATH),html:multHtml,bind:(b,r)=>b.querySelectorAll("[data-r]").forEach(c=>c.onclick=()=>{const x=+c.dataset.r,k=+c.dataset.c;multSel=multSel[0]===x&&multSel[1]===k?[0,0]:[x,k];r()})},sq:{name:"Squares and cubes chart",std:["8.EE.A.2","8.G.B.7","A-REI.4","G-SRT.8"],html:sqHtml},form:{name:"Formula and definition card",std:Object.keys(FORM),html:formHtml},ex:{name:"Worked example",std:Object.keys(EX),html:exHtml},grid:{name:"Coordinate grid",std:["8.EE.C.8","8.G.B.7","8.SP.A.1","A-REI.6","S-ID.6","8.G.A.3","8.EE.B.5","8.F.A.3"],html:pgridHtml,bind:pgridBind}};const SUP_CAVEATS=[{t:"listen",std:READ_STD,msg:"If the computer read the text aloud, this stop shows listening comprehension, not reading."},{t:"calc",std:EXT_MATH,msg:"The calculator does the arithmetic. Rate whether the student set the problem up correctly, read the rate off the right thing, and carried the right unit."},{t:"mult",std:EXT_MATH,msg:"The multiplication chart supplied the facts. Rate the setup and the reasoning."},{t:"form",std:EXT_MATH,msg:"The method card states the exponent rules, what a function is, what m and b mean, and every transformation rule this stop asks for, so these answers show applying a stated method rather than recalling it. Note it."},{t:"grid",std:["8.G.A.3"],msg:"If the image was plotted on the grid rather than worked out from the coordinates, note it. The standard asks the student to describe the effect using coordinates."},{t:"frames",std:["RI.8.8"],msg:"The frames name claim, reason and evidence for the student, so count the sorting part of the answer as supported and rate the judgment about soundness and relevance."},{t:"passage",msg:"If the text or the table was read to the student, this stop shows listening comprehension, not reading."},{t:"terms",std:READ_STD,msg:"The reading terms card defines cite, inference, connotation, relevant, and the difference between a fact and an interpretation, so this stop then shows applying those ideas with a reference."},{t:"frames",msg:"Answer frames give the sentence structure. Rate the evidence and the thinking the student supplied."},{t:"frames",std:["RI.8.9"],msg:"The frames for this stop name the two kinds of disagreement, fact and interpretation, so count the sorting part of the answer as supported and rate the evidence the student found."},{t:"frames",std:["8.SP.A.1","S-ID.6"],msg:"The frames name association and outlier for the student, so count the vocabulary of the answer as supported and rate the reading of the plot."},{t:"gram",std:["L.8.1","L.9-10.1"],msg:"The grammar card defines verbals, voice, mood, and phrase types with examples, so this stop shows using a reference, not recall."},{t:"plan",msg:"The planner supplies the organization, so count the organization check as supported, not independent."},{t:"plan",std:["W.8.1","W.9-10.1"],msg:"The planner includes a line for the other side's claim, so count the counterclaim check as supported."},{t:"sent",msg:"Sentence frames supply the linking words and the closing, so count those checks as supported."},{t:"sent",std:["W.8.1"],msg:"One of the frames shows how to report a disputed figure, so count that check as supported if the student used it."},{t:"check",msg:"The checklist names what to include. Note that the student used it to revise."},{t:"calc",std:["8.EE.C.8","A-REI.6","8.NS.A.1"],msg:"The calculator does the arithmetic. Rate the setup and the method, not the computation."},{t:"calc",std:["8.G.B.7","G-SRT.8","8.EE.A.2","A-REI.4"],msg:"The calculator has a square root key, so rate whether the student knew which squares to add or subtract, not the root itself."},{t:"calc",std:["8.SP.A.1","S-ID.6"],msg:"The calculator worked out the slope. Rate whether the student knew what to compute and what the result shows."},{t:"sq",std:["8.EE.A.2","8.G.B.7","A-REI.4","G-SRT.8"],msg:"The squares and cubes chart gives the roots, so count the final step of each question as supported and rate the setup."},{t:"form",std:["8.EE.C.8","A-REI.6"],msg:"The card lists the methods and the three cases for the number of solutions, so count the “how many solutions” question as supported."},{t:"form",std:["8.EE.A.2","A-REI.4"],msg:"The card states that a square equation has two solutions and a cube equation one, and that a length cannot be negative, so count those answers as supported."},{t:"form",std:["8.NS.A.1"],msg:"The card gives the definitions and the subtraction method for a repeating decimal, so this stop then shows applying a stated rule rather than recalling it."},{t:"form",std:["8.G.B.7","G-SRT.8"],msg:"The card gives the theorem, the rule for a leg, the three-dimensional version, and the trigonometric ratios, so this stop shows applying them with a reference."},{t:"form",std:["8.SP.A.1","S-ID.6"],msg:"The card names what to look for on a scatter plot, says an outlier should be reported rather than deleted, and warns about extrapolation, so count those judgments as supported."},{t:"ex",msg:"A worked example models the method. Count the matching questions as supported."},{t:"grid",std:["8.EE.C.8","A-REI.6"],msg:"If the pair of equations was solved by plotting on the grid tool, note that the student solved by graphing rather than by algebra."},{t:"grid",std:["8.G.B.7"],msg:"If the grid tool was used to count the horizontal and vertical legs, note that the student read them off a picture rather than subtracting the coordinates."},{t:"scribe",msg:"If an adult wrote, rate the ideas and the organization. Do not rate conventions the adult supplied."},{t:"fewer",msg:"If questions were skipped, change the “of” number in the score to the number the student tried."},{t:"hint",msg:"Note which question got the hint, and count that question as supported."}];return{stops:STOPS,levelups:LEVELUPS,tools:TOOLS,supCaveats:SUP_CAVEATS,logNames:LOG_NAMES,stopLogs,onToolChange:()=>stopSpeak()}}});
